WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.409
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.098 --> 00:00:14.640
COHEN: Hello.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.980
Hello.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.500
I'm Josh Cohen.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.070
I teach in the linguistics and
philosophy department here.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.040
And I'm the head of the
Political Science Department.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:34.050
And I'm up here now because
I'm the chair of the Colloquium

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:37.500
Committee that organized
this two day event.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:42.060
I'd like to welcome all of you
to this first session of a two

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:47.070
day colloquium entitled,
Asking the Right Questions,

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:50.940
in which we celebrate the
50th anniversary of MIT

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.980
School of Humanities,
Arts, and Social Sciences.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.170
Since 1950, the
school has been home

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:01.470
to an extraordinary collection
of scholarly activities,

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:06.330
of composers and economists,
linguists and literary critics,

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.960
historians, anthropologists,
philosophers, and many others,

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:14.070
who pursue topics ranging
from the analytical study

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:18.180
of human language, market
exchange and group conflict,

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.120
to the interpretive examination
of texts, histories,

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.170
and cultures, to the imaginative
exploration of human passions

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.480
and relationships.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.338
It's an unusual school and
an unusual institution.

00:01:31.338 --> 00:01:32.880
And our hope is that
you'll come away

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.470
from these two
days with a deeper

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.080
sense of the
contribution to knowledge

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.150
that comes from the school.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:43.500
Not so much preoccupied with
CP Snow about whether there

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.820
are two cultures, or three
cultures, or six, or nine,

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.910
in the scholarly world,
but instead engaged

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:56.070
by the questions that we're
going to be discussing here.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.437
Before proceeding,
I want to express

00:01:58.437 --> 00:02:00.270
thanks to the other
members of the committee

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:04.050
that organized the event,
Colleagues at MIT--

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.830
Pete Donaldson, Ross
Williams, Alan Brody,

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.300
and Olivier Blanchard.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.330
But I particularly want
to take the opportunity

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.980
to thank Phil Khoury, who bears
substantial responsibility

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.310
for making this
colloquium come off, bear

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.170
substantial responsibility
for everything

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:27.260
that moves around
this side of campus,

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.700
and I think more
fundamentally is really

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.740
a person, as everyone
who knows him

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:35.640
knows, of extraordinary decency,
a great pleasure to work with.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.860
And I'd like to thank
him on behalf of everyone

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.390
at MIT for all that he
does for the institute.

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:43.536
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:51.450
All that he does
at the institute,

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:54.330
including, if I may say, until
about 11 o'clock this morning

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:57.660
sending me instructions about
what I should be saying now.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.510
But I needed the instructions.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:04.560
I welcome it.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.930
So this two day
colloquium is one

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:12.120
of four parts of the 50th
anniversary celebration.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.630
A second part is an
exhibition, which

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:18.720
opened last month in MIT's
Compton Gallery in building

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:22.400
10 called, A 50 Year
Reflection on the Humanities,

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.130
Arts, and Social
Sciences at MIT.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:27.660
As I say, it opened last month.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.990
It's going to continue
until the end of January.

00:03:29.990 --> 00:03:33.600
But it will be open this
weekend, special hours.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.630
And the Nobel Prize
medals will be on display.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.890
But don't get any bright ideas.

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.040
There's an MIT
officer who's going

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:46.560
to be guarding the medals.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.350
Another part of
the four part event

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:52.020
are some concerts
tonight at Kresge,

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.870
beginning at the
Kresge oval at 7:15

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:57.720
with a performance
directed by Evan Ziporyn.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:59.430
And then in Kresge
auditorium at 8:00,

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:03.840
a concert with performances
by the MIT Wind Ensemble,

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:06.720
the Chamber Music Society,
Festival Jazz Ensemble,

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.720
symphony orchestra,
and concert choir.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:13.260
And that concert will
celebrate, among other things,

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:18.120
the arts at MIT and the addition
of the arts to our masthead,

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:21.462
as in School of Humanities,
Arts, and Social Sciences.

00:04:21.462 --> 00:04:23.670
And then the final event is
the dinner tomorrow night

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:27.330
at the Boston
Museum of Fine Arts.

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.730
Now, as to the
colloquium itself,

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:31.830
beginning about a
year and a half ago,

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.200
Phil Khoury organized
a series of discussions

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:38.790
about how we might celebrate
our 50th anniversary.

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:41.340
And a consensus emerged
through those discussions,

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:43.440
that the right way
to do this was not

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:48.390
so much to applaud past efforts,
or celebrate past achievements,

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:53.250
but instead to have an event in
which we do what we do when we

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:57.300
do it well, that is to
think, talk, and argue

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:01.530
about large themes of
real human importance.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:06.680
Time and world permitting,
everyone in the school

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:09.470
would have a chance to
discuss his or her work.

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:13.340
But Andrew Marvell was
right, both time and world

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:16.490
are in unhappily scarce supply.

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:20.390
So we decided to focus
on four large themes that

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:25.550
have been persistent foci
of attention of humanists,

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:28.160
artists, and social
scientists over the 50 year

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:31.440
life of the school.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:36.110
So we have four topics, each
defined by a broad question.

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:39.465
And I'll just state
the questions,

00:05:39.465 --> 00:05:41.090
say who's going to
be speaking to them,

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:46.680
and leave to the panels
themselves and the chairs

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.020
of the panels the
introduction of the themes

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:51.290
in more detail and the
introduction of the speaker.

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:56.700
So this session, what do
we know about human nature?

00:05:56.700 --> 00:06:02.140
Will go from,
well, 1:15 to 3:00,

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:09.130
or so, with Noam Chomsky, Steve
Pinker, and Hilary Putnam.

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:11.530
Then, after a short
break, at 3:30,

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:17.140
we start again with a topic, how
do artists tell their stories?

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:20.020
With three artists who tell
stories in different media,

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:23.950
composer John Harbison,
novelist Anita Desai,

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:26.920
and poet Louise Gluck.

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:29.860
Then tomorrow at 9:30,
how do history and memory

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:31.120
shape each other?

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:35.440
With John Dower, Pauline
Mair, and Dame Gillian Beer.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:38.500
And we finish tomorrow at 1
o'clock with the question,

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:40.960
is capitalism good
for democracy?

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:44.800
With Suzanne Berger, Bob
Solow, and Ken Arrow.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:46.840
And I want to take this
opportunity to thank all

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:48.940
the participants on
all the sessions,

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:54.370
but particularly Hillary-- was
my teacher in graduate school--

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:57.160
Gillian Beer, Ken
Arrow, and Louise Gluck

00:06:57.160 --> 00:06:59.770
for joining us on
this special occasion.

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:01.840
All the sessions, as
you probably know,

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:02.990
will be in this room.

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:06.100
And as you may not know,
for those who come tomorrow,

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:09.640
lunch will be served in the
faculty club between sessions.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:13.653
And on that mundane
but essential detail,

00:07:13.653 --> 00:07:15.070
I'll hand things
over to my friend

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:18.490
Jay Keyser who is the
Peter de Florez Professor

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:20.620
of Linguistics, Emeritus.

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:24.580
And Jay will introduce
our first question.

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:25.510
Jay.

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:26.010
Thank you.

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:37.150
KEYSER: Welcome to the School
of Humanities, Arts, and Social

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:40.196
Sciences version of
The Three Tenors.

00:07:49.630 --> 00:07:52.220
I will be your moderator
for this session.

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:54.590
You can tell who I am.

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:58.400
I'm the one with the longest
name on the name tags there.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:01.270
My name is Samuel Jay Keyser.

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:07.120
So I'll say a word about how
the event will be structured

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:10.020
and then we'll begin.

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:14.060
So what I'll do is
introduce each speaker.

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:20.090
And they'll speak in the
order of Steve Pinker and then

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:22.820
Hilary Putnam and
then Noam Chomsky.

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:26.730
Each speaker will speak
for about 20 minutes,

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:30.300
and then we'll take
questions from all of you.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:33.220
And as Josh says, we should
go up to about 3 o'clock.

00:08:38.049 --> 00:08:40.230
So I thought I
might try to lay out

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:45.760
a kind of a context
for the discussion.

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:52.210
In 1958, as a graduate student
in linguistics at Yale,

00:08:52.210 --> 00:08:54.070
I was taught that
the study of language

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:57.830
was the study of finite corpora.

00:08:57.830 --> 00:09:00.020
The job of the linguist
was to choose a language,

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:03.140
collect a finite, if large,
corpus of that language

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:05.000
and describe it.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:07.940
I remember that one of my
old mentors, Bernard Bloch,

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:11.480
used to refer to that
exercise as collecting

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:17.810
your 40 notebooks of Choctaw
and then describing it.

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:19.700
Three years later,
I came to MIT.

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:22.630
Here I learned something
radically different.

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:27.970
"Language is a process of free
creation, and I'm quoting,

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:30.440
its laws and
principles are fixed,

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:32.630
but the manner in which the
principles of generation

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:39.336
are used is free and infinitely
varied," end of quote.

00:09:39.336 --> 00:09:42.250
The quotation is
from Noam Chomsky

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:45.880
and the import is that language,
an important, perhaps the most

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:48.640
important aspect
of human nature,

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:54.090
is quintessentially infinite
creativity within a finite set

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:56.310
of boundaries.

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:59.190
Now, this point of view often
glimpsed in the 19th century

00:09:59.190 --> 00:10:03.690
by critics such as Wilhelm
von Humboldt, or Samuel Taylor

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:07.290
Coleridge, and
Friedrich von Schlegel,

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:09.475
was elevated to the
level of a science at MIT

00:10:09.475 --> 00:10:10.725
in the middle of this century.

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:15.710
Creativity, within
fixed boundaries,

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:18.350
goes beyond Homo sapiens.

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:22.010
It is a reflection of a
fundamental property of life.

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:24.980
Unity, IE, fixed boundaries
within diversity,

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:30.460
IE, creativity, are two
sides of the same coin.

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:33.630
The 450 different
species of birds

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:37.110
that live on the shores
of Lake Baringo in Kenya

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:41.690
are, nonetheless, cut
from the same cloth.

00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:44.960
The thousands of varieties
of flowers are, in a sense,

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:47.240
a single flower.

00:10:47.240 --> 00:10:50.780
The faces of humankind, as
varied as they seem to be,

00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:56.700
are, nonetheless,
variations on a single face.

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:59.640
The thousands of natural
languages in the world

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:03.750
are, at some level,
the same language.

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:05.920
From the perspective
of unity and diversity,

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:09.600
then nothing could be more
natural than language.

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:12.960
From the perspective of
human nature, what is special

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:17.850
is the element of freedom that
accompanies our mental life

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:21.150
as it is mirrored in language.

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:25.640
Well, that'll be
sort of the context.

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:29.700
And I'd like to go immediately
to our first speaker,

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:32.890
and that is Steve Pinker.

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:36.970
By the way, I'm the emeritus
Peter de Florez professor.

00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:39.800
Steve is the current
holder of it.

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:41.320
So in a way, I'm a
surrogate father.

00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:45.130
Don't stay out late, Steve.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:55.360
It's a bit frightening
to think that I've

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:59.765
known Noam and Hilary longer
than our first panelist has

00:11:59.765 --> 00:12:00.265
been alive.

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:06.370
I guess, that's
the kind of thing

00:12:06.370 --> 00:12:11.740
you have to expect when you
celebrate 50 years of anything.

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:14.900
If careers where
music compositions,

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:18.250
Steve's would be
the 1812 Overture

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:22.150
with the fireworks reserved for
his spectacular series of books

00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:26.630
aimed at the interested but
uninformed general public.

00:12:26.630 --> 00:12:31.070
In 1994, The Language
Instinct, his first book,

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:33.170
was named 1 of the 11
best books of the year

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:35.240
by The New York
Times Book Review

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:38.450
and it won the William James
Book Prize of the American

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:39.800
Psychological Association.

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:44.900
In fact, he's the recipient of
2 William James Book Prizes,

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:47.450
as well as the Los
Angeles Times Book Prize

00:12:47.450 --> 00:12:50.280
in Science and Technology.

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:51.710
He was a finalist
for the Pulitzer

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:54.510
Prize in nonfiction in the
National Book Critics Award

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:55.070
Circle--

00:12:55.070 --> 00:12:58.160
Circle Award he should have won.

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:01.010
Many have rested on the laurels
of the likes of the language

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:02.540
instinct, not Steve.

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:06.500
He followed that book up with
How The Mind Works in 1997,

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:09.350
and Words and
Rules in 1999, each

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:11.690
a tour de force
in its own right.

00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:14.180
And he's currently
working on yet another

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:16.520
to be called The Blank Slate.

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:20.300
Steve is on a roll,
one which I strongly

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:23.780
suspect will go on
for the next 50 years.

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:24.936
Steve Pinker.

00:13:24.936 --> 00:13:28.904
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:28.904 --> 00:13:29.896
PINKER: Thanks, Jay.

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:43.300
Thank you very much, Jay.

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:44.410
Thank you, Josh.

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:47.560
And thank you, Phil, and
all of you for coming.

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:49.600
The topic of this
afternoon's symposium

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:52.360
is, of course, of
perennial importance,

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:56.140
because everyone has a
theory of human nature.

00:13:56.140 --> 00:13:58.660
Everyone has to predict
how other people will

00:13:58.660 --> 00:14:00.430
react to their surroundings.

00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:02.140
And that means that
we all depend on

00:14:02.140 --> 00:14:06.820
theories, explicit or implicit,
on what makes people tick.

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:09.130
Much depends on our
theory of human nature.

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:12.670
It affects everything from how
we manage our relationships

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:14.110
and how we bring
up our children,

00:14:14.110 --> 00:14:19.600
to our theories of
education, politics, and law.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:22.750
For thousands of years, the
dominant theory of human nature

00:14:22.750 --> 00:14:25.090
was the Judeo-Christian
tradition,

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:27.710
which had claims on
the subject matter

00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:30.370
now covered by
psychology and biology.

00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:33.850
For example, it proposed
a modular theory of mind

00:14:33.850 --> 00:14:37.150
in which the mind contains
a moral sense which

00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:40.450
can recognize standards of good
and evil and uncaused decision

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:44.050
making process and
a capacity for love.

00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:46.960
The Judeo-Christian theory
was based on specific events

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:48.220
narrated in the Bible.

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:52.060
For example, the,
we know that people

00:14:52.060 --> 00:14:55.480
have the capacity for choice
because Adam and Eve were

00:14:55.480 --> 00:14:58.330
punished for eating the fruit of
the tree of knowledge implying

00:14:58.330 --> 00:15:00.970
that they could have
chosen otherwise.

00:15:00.970 --> 00:15:03.700
With the decline of
literal interpretations

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:06.730
of the truth of biblical events,
the Judeo-Christian theory

00:15:06.730 --> 00:15:11.350
of human nature has been
dissolving among intellectuals.

00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:15.380
But since a theory of
human nature is necessary,

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:17.533
a new one had to take its place.

00:15:17.533 --> 00:15:19.450
I'm going to suggest
that the standard secular

00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:22.420
theory of human nature that's
been with us until recently

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:26.230
is composed of three
doctrines, each associated

00:15:26.230 --> 00:15:28.270
with a modern philosopher.

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:30.070
I should hasten to
add that I'm going

00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:34.240
to be using these philosophers
really as hooks for the ideas.

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:37.480
And it should go without
saying that their actual bodies

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:39.340
of thought are far
more sophisticated

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:42.050
than the sound bites that
I'll present you today.

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:44.620
The first is The Blank
Slate, or tabula rasa,

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:48.520
literally scraped tablet,
often incorrectly attributed

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:50.090
to John Locke.

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:52.460
What he really said
was as follows,

00:15:52.460 --> 00:15:54.820
let us suppose then the
mind to be, as we say,

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:58.780
white paper, void of all
characters, without any ideas.

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:00.460
How comes it to be furnished?

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:02.320
When comes it by
that vast store,

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:04.690
which the busy and
boundless fancy of man

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:07.570
has painted on it with an
almost endless variety?

00:16:07.570 --> 00:16:10.690
Whence has it all the materials
of reason and knowledge?

00:16:10.690 --> 00:16:14.480
To that, I answer in one
word, from experience.

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:16.840
It's easy to see why
The Blank Slate should

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:19.060
have been an appealing
doctrine in Locke's time,

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:21.160
as it is today.

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:23.680
Dogmas, such as the
divine right of kings,

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:25.990
are not self-evident
truths, but have

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:31.060
to be justified by experiences
that minds can share.

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:33.850
If ideas come from experience,
then differences of opinion

00:16:33.850 --> 00:16:37.343
come from different experiences,
not from defective minds.

00:16:37.343 --> 00:16:39.760
And therefore, at least in the
conventional interpretation

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:42.280
of Locke, it gives us grounds
for tolerating differences

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:44.050
of opinion.

00:16:44.050 --> 00:16:48.040
If all of us are blank slates,
this undermines the notion

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:52.060
that the royalty and aristocracy
have some innate advantage

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:54.880
and suitability to
rule, and conversely, it

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:57.010
undermines slavery
by saying that slaves

00:16:57.010 --> 00:17:00.100
are not innately
inferior, or subservient.

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:02.440
The Blank Slate has
had an enormous impact

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:04.990
on contemporary
intellectual life.

00:17:04.990 --> 00:17:07.270
Psychology for much
of this century

00:17:07.270 --> 00:17:09.910
has devoted itself to the
study of the simple mechanisms

00:17:09.910 --> 00:17:12.819
of association that
Locke first described.

00:17:12.819 --> 00:17:16.599
The social sciences have invoked
culture and socialization

00:17:16.599 --> 00:17:19.444
as the primary
explanatory constructs.

00:17:19.444 --> 00:17:21.069
I'm going to give
you an example of how

00:17:21.069 --> 00:17:25.839
the idea has penetrated into
another famous 20th century

00:17:25.839 --> 00:17:26.920
philosopher.

00:17:26.920 --> 00:17:29.272
I think of a child's
mind as a blank book.

00:17:29.272 --> 00:17:30.730
During the first
years of its life,

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:32.710
much will be written
on the pages.

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:35.890
The quality of that writing
will affect his life profoundly.

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:37.450
Who said that?

00:17:37.450 --> 00:17:40.210
Walt Disney.

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:42.430
Now, the second
doctrine was first

00:17:42.430 --> 00:17:45.370
introduced in the
following verse

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:48.490
by John Dryden in The
Conquest of Granada.

00:17:48.490 --> 00:17:50.410
I am as free as
nature first made

00:17:50.410 --> 00:17:52.600
man, ere the base
laws of servitude

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:56.410
began, when wild in woods
the noble savage ran.

00:17:56.410 --> 00:17:59.050
The expression the noble
savages incorrectly

00:17:59.050 --> 00:18:02.560
attributed to Rousseau,
although he did

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:04.370
write something quite similar.

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:06.670
So many authors have
hastily concluded

00:18:06.670 --> 00:18:08.800
that man is naturally
cruel and requires

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:12.160
a regular system of police to be
reclaimed, whereas nothing can

00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:14.680
be more gentle than him
in his primitive state

00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:16.810
when placed by nature
at an equal distance

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:19.420
from the stupidity of brutes
and the pernicious good sense

00:18:19.420 --> 00:18:21.220
of civilized man.

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:23.440
The more we reflect on this
state, the more convinced

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:25.607
we shall be that it was the
least subject of any two

00:18:25.607 --> 00:18:28.120
revolutions, the best
for man, and that nothing

00:18:28.120 --> 00:18:30.910
could have drawn him out of
it, but some fatal accident,

00:18:30.910 --> 00:18:33.550
which for the public good,
should never have happened.

00:18:33.550 --> 00:18:35.620
The example of the
savages, most of whom

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:37.120
have been found
in this condition,

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:39.460
seems to confirm that
mankind was formed ever

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:41.410
to remain in it, that
this condition is

00:18:41.410 --> 00:18:44.200
the real youth of the
world, and that all ulterior

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:47.230
improvements have been so
many steps in appearance

00:18:47.230 --> 00:18:50.170
towards the perfection of
individuals, but, in fact,

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:53.020
towards the decrepitness
of the species.

00:18:53.020 --> 00:18:55.450
It's easy to see the appeal
of the doctrine of The Noble

00:18:55.450 --> 00:18:57.430
Savage, that we
are innately good

00:18:57.430 --> 00:19:02.360
and that our social institutions
cause us to do wrong.

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:04.630
There is no need for the
domineering Leviathan,

00:19:04.630 --> 00:19:08.910
that Hobbes, Rousseau's foil
in that passage, proposed.

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:11.920
If we're nasty, our best
hope is an uneasy truce,

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:13.600
whereas if we're
essentially noble,

00:19:13.600 --> 00:19:17.320
a happy society is our
birthright, a cheerier thought.

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:19.390
Children, in a sense,
are born savages.

00:19:19.390 --> 00:19:22.390
So if the savage in us is
nasty, then child rearing

00:19:22.390 --> 00:19:24.760
is an arena of
discipline and conflict,

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:27.640
whereas if the savage
is noble, child rearing

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:29.920
consists of providing
children with opportunities

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:31.910
to develop their potential.

00:19:31.910 --> 00:19:34.510
Finally, it'd be nice
to think that evil

00:19:34.510 --> 00:19:36.340
is the product of
a corrupt society

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:40.240
rather than a dark side
that's insufficiently tamed.

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:42.040
The Noble Savage,
like The Blank Slate,

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:47.200
has a remarkable degree of
penetration in our unspoken

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:48.920
theory of human nature.

00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:51.970
I think it can be seen in
the respect for the natural

00:19:51.970 --> 00:19:54.610
and distrust of the man
made that manifests itself

00:19:54.610 --> 00:19:58.060
in many arenas, such as the
opposition to genetically

00:19:58.060 --> 00:20:02.290
modified food, the unfashioned
ability of authoritarian styles

00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:04.420
of child rearing,
and the understanding

00:20:04.420 --> 00:20:07.780
of social problems as repairable
defects in our institutions,

00:20:07.780 --> 00:20:10.270
something that virtually
all of us share,

00:20:10.270 --> 00:20:12.130
as opposed to an
older view that they

00:20:12.130 --> 00:20:15.830
are part of the inherent
tragedy of the human condition.

00:20:15.830 --> 00:20:19.690
The third doctrine
is commonly linked

00:20:19.690 --> 00:20:23.040
with Descartes, the notion
of mind, body dualism.

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:24.850
And again, I will
give you a sound bite

00:20:24.850 --> 00:20:28.570
that doesn't do real justice
to his body of thought.

00:20:28.570 --> 00:20:30.550
When I consider the
mind, that is to say,

00:20:30.550 --> 00:20:33.340
myself in as much as I
am only a thinking being,

00:20:33.340 --> 00:20:35.440
I cannot distinguish
in myself any parts,

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:38.520
but apprehend myself to
be clearly one and entire.

00:20:38.520 --> 00:20:41.500
The faculties of willing,
feeling, conceiving, et cetera,

00:20:41.500 --> 00:20:44.300
cannot be properly speaking
said to be its parts,

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:47.020
for it is one in the same mind,
which employs itself in willing

00:20:47.020 --> 00:20:49.000
and in feeling and
in understanding.

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:52.660
But it is quite otherwise with
corporeal, or extended objects,

00:20:52.660 --> 00:20:55.670
for as there is not one
of them imaginable by me,

00:20:55.670 --> 00:20:58.390
which my mind cannot
easily divide into parts.

00:20:58.390 --> 00:20:59.890
This would be
sufficient to teach me

00:20:59.890 --> 00:21:02.200
that the mind or soul
of man is entirely

00:21:02.200 --> 00:21:03.940
different from the body.

00:21:03.940 --> 00:21:06.610
The Ghost in the Machine
is the abusive term

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:10.390
for this doctrine introduced
by Gilbert Ryle 50 years ago.

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:12.460
And it's easy to see
why the notion that we

00:21:12.460 --> 00:21:15.700
are suffused with some
non-mechanical spirit

00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:17.110
should be appealing.

00:21:17.110 --> 00:21:20.950
Machines, such as the
body is composed of,

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:24.010
are insensate, built to
be used and disposable.

00:21:24.010 --> 00:21:26.860
Humans are sentient, possessing
of dignity and rights,

00:21:26.860 --> 00:21:28.780
and infinitely precious.

00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:30.700
Machines have some
workaday purpose,

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:33.340
like grinding corn,
or sharpening pencils.

00:21:33.340 --> 00:21:36.520
Humans have a higher purpose,
love, worship, good works,

00:21:36.520 --> 00:21:38.500
knowledge, beauty, and so on.

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:40.440
Machines follow the
laws of physics,

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:42.760
behavior is freely chosen.

00:21:42.760 --> 00:21:45.370
With choice comes optimism
about our possibilities

00:21:45.370 --> 00:21:48.880
for the future and the
ability to hold others

00:21:48.880 --> 00:21:51.290
accountable for their actions.

00:21:51.290 --> 00:21:54.400
Finally, given the doctrine
of The Ghost in the Machine,

00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:56.900
the mind can survive
the death of the body,

00:21:56.900 --> 00:22:00.190
an idea whose appeal
should be all too obvious.

00:22:00.190 --> 00:22:02.260
Again, I think this
is a doctrine that

00:22:02.260 --> 00:22:07.040
pervades our common sense
theories of human nature.

00:22:07.040 --> 00:22:09.490
We see it in the fact that
freedom, choice, dignity,

00:22:09.490 --> 00:22:11.350
responsibility, and
rights are commonly

00:22:11.350 --> 00:22:15.830
seen as incompatible with a
biological view of the mind.

00:22:15.830 --> 00:22:19.000
We see it in the common use of
determinism and reductionism

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:24.190
as boo words, as words that
no one ever really explains

00:22:24.190 --> 00:22:25.690
what they mean by
them, but everyone

00:22:25.690 --> 00:22:27.590
knows that they're bad.

00:22:27.590 --> 00:22:29.680
And in everyday
thinking and speech

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:33.280
you often can see speculation
about brain transplants.

00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:35.530
In reality, they would
be body transplants,

00:22:35.530 --> 00:22:37.750
because as Dan
Dennett pointed out,

00:22:37.750 --> 00:22:39.825
the brain transplant
is the one transplant

00:22:39.825 --> 00:22:41.200
operation where
it's better to be

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:44.080
the donor than the recipient.

00:22:44.080 --> 00:22:45.880
And we see it in
everyday language

00:22:45.880 --> 00:22:48.250
as when we refer to
John's body, or even

00:22:48.250 --> 00:22:51.910
John's brain, which
presupposes some entity that

00:22:51.910 --> 00:22:56.490
owns the brain, and yet is
somehow distinct from it.

00:22:56.490 --> 00:22:58.260
I think over the
last 50 years there

00:22:58.260 --> 00:23:00.690
have been a number of threats
to each of these doctrines.

00:23:00.690 --> 00:23:02.280
And in the rest
of my remarks I'd

00:23:02.280 --> 00:23:04.650
like to indicate
what they are and how

00:23:04.650 --> 00:23:06.750
we might deal with them.

00:23:06.750 --> 00:23:09.990
The foremost, I think, is
the Chomskyan Revolution

00:23:09.990 --> 00:23:11.880
in linguistics and
cognitive science,

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:16.200
where Noam in the
1950s first proposed

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:20.160
that, quite contrary to the
doctrine of the blank slate,

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:23.190
every normal child is
fitted with a language

00:23:23.190 --> 00:23:25.950
faculty, a language
acquisition device, which

00:23:25.950 --> 00:23:28.650
is necessary for the
learning of languages

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:31.710
to take place, no learning
without innate learning

00:23:31.710 --> 00:23:32.760
algorithms.

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:34.410
And in the rest of
cognitive science,

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:36.210
there have been
parallel proposals

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:39.810
for learning circuitry,
which must be there in order

00:23:39.810 --> 00:23:42.030
for learning to happen.

00:23:42.030 --> 00:23:45.210
In developmental psychology,
my colleague Elizabeth Spelke

00:23:45.210 --> 00:23:48.240
and others have shown
that infants in the crib

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:53.340
have a remarkable degree of
cognitive and psychological

00:23:53.340 --> 00:23:55.470
faculties operating
just about as

00:23:55.470 --> 00:23:58.200
soon as their brain
has developed.

00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:02.160
In behavioral genetics, the
study of adoptees and twins,

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:05.310
there is evidence that
about half the variation

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:08.940
in intellectual skills
and in personality traits

00:24:08.940 --> 00:24:11.610
can be tied to
differences in the genes.

00:24:11.610 --> 00:24:15.780
And evolutionary psychology has
documented an astonishing list

00:24:15.780 --> 00:24:19.080
of traits that are universal
across the world, 6,000

00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:21.510
cultures, just as
Noam has proposed

00:24:21.510 --> 00:24:24.210
a universal grammar, the
anthropologist Donald

00:24:24.210 --> 00:24:26.820
Brown influenced by
Chomsky has proposed

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:30.810
a universal people, a set of
traits that includes everything

00:24:30.810 --> 00:24:35.520
from logical operators in
language to arranging of hair

00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:41.490
for aesthetic reasons to sexual
and child rearing practices.

00:24:41.490 --> 00:24:45.660
And primatology has shown
that our hairy cousins have

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:48.060
a number of
psychological abilities,

00:24:48.060 --> 00:24:50.880
such as a concept of
number and many emotions

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:54.540
and facial expressions,
despite their lacking culture

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:58.740
and socialization processes,
as we see in people.

00:24:58.740 --> 00:25:01.080
The Noble Savage, I think,
has also been threatened.

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:04.590
Behavioral genetics has shown
that many of the nasty traits

00:25:04.590 --> 00:25:07.230
that we find in people,
such as antagonism,

00:25:07.230 --> 00:25:10.500
a lack of conscience, a
tendency towards violent crime,

00:25:10.500 --> 00:25:14.190
and psychopathy, are
partly heritable.

00:25:14.190 --> 00:25:17.190
Neuroscience has identified
brain mechanisms associated

00:25:17.190 --> 00:25:19.110
with aggression, as
well as brain mechanisms

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:20.250
that inhibit it.

00:25:20.250 --> 00:25:22.980
And evolutionary
psychology and anthropology

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:25.620
have documented
that conflict is as

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:27.510
universal in the
human species as it

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:29.800
is in the rest of
the natural world.

00:25:29.800 --> 00:25:31.570
This is my one graph of data.

00:25:31.570 --> 00:25:33.270
It shows the percentage
of male deaths

00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:38.040
due to warfare in a variety
of hunter gatherer and hunter

00:25:38.040 --> 00:25:40.830
horticultural societies
indicated in red, mostly

00:25:40.830 --> 00:25:43.890
in the Amazon and in the
New Guinea highlands.

00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:46.050
For comparison, the
blue bar at the bottom

00:25:46.050 --> 00:25:48.060
shows the same
statistic percentage

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:51.690
of male deaths due to
warfare in the US and Europe

00:25:51.690 --> 00:25:55.050
in the 20th century, including
the statistics of the two world

00:25:55.050 --> 00:25:56.385
wars.

00:25:56.385 --> 00:25:57.900
So much for Rousseau.

00:26:00.490 --> 00:26:02.460
And I think The
Ghost in the Machine

00:26:02.460 --> 00:26:04.170
has also come under threat.

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:08.010
Cognitive science is based
on the computational theory

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:10.590
of mind, a theory, I
think, first most clearly

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:14.040
articulated by Hilary Putnam
in a famous 1960 paper.

00:26:14.040 --> 00:26:19.230
Although Hilary is now one
of the severest critics

00:26:19.230 --> 00:26:20.460
of that original approach.

00:26:20.460 --> 00:26:23.250
Nonetheless, I think it's
still the dominant approach

00:26:23.250 --> 00:26:24.720
in cognitive
science and it tries

00:26:24.720 --> 00:26:28.920
to explain the airy immaterial
processes of thinking

00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:32.310
and feeling in terms of
the mechanical notion

00:26:32.310 --> 00:26:35.760
of computation, that beliefs
are a kind of information,

00:26:35.760 --> 00:26:37.440
thinking is a kind
of computation,

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:41.040
and the emotions are a kind of
feedback and control mechanism.

00:26:41.040 --> 00:26:44.130
In neuroscience, also the
ghost has been under assault,

00:26:44.130 --> 00:26:45.990
Francis Crick
wrote a book called

00:26:45.990 --> 00:26:49.170
The Astonishing Hypothesis,
alluding to the discovery

00:26:49.170 --> 00:26:53.070
that all aspects of thinking,
feeling, and perception, as far

00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:57.300
as we can tell, can be linked
to the physiological activity

00:26:57.300 --> 00:27:00.250
of tissue of the
tissues of the brain.

00:27:00.250 --> 00:27:04.470
Now, these threats to the
three secular doctrines

00:27:04.470 --> 00:27:06.870
have not been met
with equanimity,

00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:08.700
and there have been
hostile reactions

00:27:08.700 --> 00:27:12.180
to these developments
of the last 50 years

00:27:12.180 --> 00:27:13.943
from both the left
and the right.

00:27:13.943 --> 00:27:15.360
From the academic
left, there have

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:19.500
been accusations of racism,
sexism, and Nazism of people,

00:27:19.500 --> 00:27:23.580
who are clearly none of those,
for espousing refutations

00:27:23.580 --> 00:27:25.290
of the three doctrines.

00:27:25.290 --> 00:27:27.810
Just as one nostalgic
example, here's a poster

00:27:27.810 --> 00:27:31.110
from the mid 1980s coming
here Edward L. Wilson,

00:27:31.110 --> 00:27:34.350
sociobiologist, and the prophet
of right wing patriarchy.

00:27:34.350 --> 00:27:36.180
And if you see, the
bottom of the slide

00:27:36.180 --> 00:27:38.100
says, bring noisemakers.

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:38.850
Nice touch.

00:27:42.010 --> 00:27:45.090
The religious and cultural
right have been just as outraged

00:27:45.090 --> 00:27:49.290
with accusations of immorality
and spiritual bankruptcy.

00:27:49.290 --> 00:27:51.600
Here's a quote from the
Weekly Standard, which

00:27:51.600 --> 00:27:54.990
I think is typical of the
right wing press' reaction

00:27:54.990 --> 00:27:57.420
to the new sciences
of human nature.

00:27:57.420 --> 00:27:59.010
But evolution,
cognitive science,

00:27:59.010 --> 00:28:02.010
and neuroscience are sure
to give you the creeps,

00:28:02.010 --> 00:28:03.720
because whether a
behavior is moral,

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:07.140
whether it signifies virtue, is
a judgment that the new science

00:28:07.140 --> 00:28:09.930
and materialism in
general cannot make.

00:28:09.930 --> 00:28:12.090
In contrast, the
Judeo-Christian view

00:28:12.090 --> 00:28:13.740
says that human
beings are persons

00:28:13.740 --> 00:28:16.110
from the start endowed
with a soul created

00:28:16.110 --> 00:28:18.090
by God and infinitely precious.

00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:22.110
This is the common understanding
the new science means to undo.

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:23.790
Why the fear and loathing?

00:28:23.790 --> 00:28:25.770
Why these intemperate reactions?

00:28:25.770 --> 00:28:29.100
And how might they be addressed
by people who are legitimately

00:28:29.100 --> 00:28:32.490
concerned with the human values
they are taking to threaten?

00:28:32.490 --> 00:28:37.630
I think the fears can be
reduced to three main ones.

00:28:37.630 --> 00:28:40.800
The first is the fear that if
the mind has innate structure,

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:43.170
then different races,
sexes, or individuals could

00:28:43.170 --> 00:28:44.880
be biologically different.

00:28:44.880 --> 00:28:45.840
Blank is blank.

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:48.810
If we're all blank slates, we
are by definition identical.

00:28:48.810 --> 00:28:50.460
If something is
written on the slate,

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:52.710
different things could be
written on different slates,

00:28:52.710 --> 00:28:56.740
and that would condone
discrimination and oppression.

00:28:56.740 --> 00:28:58.450
The responses to this
are, first of all,

00:28:58.450 --> 00:29:00.990
that as the political
scientist James Flynn wrote,

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:03.960
the truth cannot be
racist, or sexist,

00:29:03.960 --> 00:29:07.230
that regardless of what the
facts turn out to be on average

00:29:07.230 --> 00:29:09.240
differences among
subsets of people,

00:29:09.240 --> 00:29:12.330
for those who are interested
in studying that sort of thing,

00:29:12.330 --> 00:29:15.660
that does not impact on the
ethical and moral decision

00:29:15.660 --> 00:29:17.450
of how we ought to treat people.

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:20.070
And in particular,
discrimination

00:29:20.070 --> 00:29:23.770
against individuals on the
basis of certain group averages,

00:29:23.770 --> 00:29:26.520
such as an entire
race or sex is simply

00:29:26.520 --> 00:29:29.130
indefensible on moral
and political grounds.

00:29:29.130 --> 00:29:31.080
A position, by the
way, that I think

00:29:31.080 --> 00:29:36.600
was eloquently articulated by
Noam Chomsky in a 1970 article

00:29:36.600 --> 00:29:39.300
called Psychology and Ideology.

00:29:39.300 --> 00:29:41.220
Second fear is that
if unpleasant traits

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:45.000
are innate, aggression,
xenophobia, sexism, race, rape,

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:47.760
harassment, and so on, that
would make them, first of all,

00:29:47.760 --> 00:29:52.540
natural, and therefore, good,
or else be unchangeable.

00:29:52.540 --> 00:29:55.440
Their responses to this, that,
first of all, the first part

00:29:55.440 --> 00:29:56.970
is a non-sequitur.

00:29:56.970 --> 00:30:01.110
Is does not imply ought, as the
philosopher G. E. Moore pointed

00:30:01.110 --> 00:30:04.480
out 100 years ago, and
conversely ought does not imply

00:30:04.480 --> 00:30:04.980
is.

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:08.040
Even if it would be nice to
think that people are basically

00:30:08.040 --> 00:30:12.450
noble savages, the niceness and
pleasantness of that thought

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:14.550
is not enough to make it true.

00:30:14.550 --> 00:30:17.520
Also, the human mind is
complex with many parts.

00:30:17.520 --> 00:30:19.710
And even if there
were ignoble motives,

00:30:19.710 --> 00:30:23.220
it doesn't mean that we're
condemned to ignoble behavior,

00:30:23.220 --> 00:30:26.760
because those motives are not
the only contents of the mind.

00:30:26.760 --> 00:30:29.580
With a complex multi-part
mind, ignoble motives

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:32.550
can be counteracted by
other motives and thoughts,

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:36.780
such as an innate moral sense,
or a cognitive recognition

00:30:36.780 --> 00:30:40.260
of the mutual benefits that
come about through cooperation.

00:30:40.260 --> 00:30:43.290
And indeed, only by
understanding our darker side

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:45.390
can we hope to
control it by trying

00:30:45.390 --> 00:30:49.540
to pit the more noble motives
against the ignoble ones.

00:30:49.540 --> 00:30:51.660
Finally, there's the
fear that if behavior

00:30:51.660 --> 00:30:55.170
is caused by a person's biology,
he can't be held responsible

00:30:55.170 --> 00:30:56.610
for it.

00:30:56.610 --> 00:30:58.920
As in, the excuse, I
can't help it, honey.

00:30:58.920 --> 00:31:03.000
I'm just following an
imperative to spread my genes.

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:04.870
I think the response
for that is,

00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:08.310
first of all, an ancient
one, that explanation is not

00:31:08.310 --> 00:31:13.200
the same as exculpation, or as
the saying goes, to understand

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:14.430
is not to forgive.

00:31:14.430 --> 00:31:17.730
It can't be true that
when we understand

00:31:17.730 --> 00:31:20.580
what makes people tick,
that we can no longer set up

00:31:20.580 --> 00:31:23.250
contingencies of
moral evaluation,

00:31:23.250 --> 00:31:26.250
of reward, and praise,
and condemnation

00:31:26.250 --> 00:31:29.700
that would, in the future,
affect that kind of behavior.

00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:32.430
Also, bogus defenses
for bad behavior

00:31:32.430 --> 00:31:34.500
are, in the 20th
century, ironically,

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:37.710
more likely to be
cultural than biological.

00:31:37.710 --> 00:31:39.480
We've had the abuse
expuse that was

00:31:39.480 --> 00:31:42.690
used to exonerate the Menendez
brothers in their first trial,

00:31:42.690 --> 00:31:45.000
the pornography made
me do it defense

00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:49.050
used by clever lawyers
of rapists and harassers,

00:31:49.050 --> 00:31:51.840
and the black rage syndrome that
William Kunstler was prepared

00:31:51.840 --> 00:31:55.080
to use to defend Colin Ferguson,
the Long Island Railroad

00:31:55.080 --> 00:31:55.950
gunman.

00:31:55.950 --> 00:31:58.380
But my favorite
example is the song

00:31:58.380 --> 00:32:02.160
by the Jets in West Side Story
that I'm sure you all remember.

00:32:02.160 --> 00:32:03.600
According to the
Sondheim lyrics,

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:06.710
the Jets sang, dear,
kindly, Sergeant Krupke, you

00:32:06.710 --> 00:32:09.570
gotta understand, it's
just our bringing up-ke

00:32:09.570 --> 00:32:11.120
that gets us out of hand.

00:32:11.120 --> 00:32:14.160
Our mothers all are junkies,
our fathers all are drunks.

00:32:14.160 --> 00:32:17.760
Golly Moses,
natcherly we're punks.

00:32:17.760 --> 00:32:21.750
So in conclusion, I think
the religious theory

00:32:21.750 --> 00:32:27.120
of human nature was replaced
within 100 years ago

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:29.670
and more by the doctrines
of The Blank Slate,

00:32:29.670 --> 00:32:33.630
The Noble Savage, and
The Ghost in the Machine.

00:32:33.630 --> 00:32:37.020
This theory is being challenged
by the cognitive revolution

00:32:37.020 --> 00:32:39.240
and the revolutions in
neuroscience, genetics,

00:32:39.240 --> 00:32:40.500
and evolution.

00:32:40.500 --> 00:32:43.350
It's seen as a threat
to deeply held values.

00:32:43.350 --> 00:32:46.290
But I would argue
that that threat

00:32:46.290 --> 00:32:48.450
is more apparent than real.

00:32:48.450 --> 00:32:51.990
On the contrary, I think
the taking human nature

00:32:51.990 --> 00:32:55.240
into account can help
us clarify these values.

00:32:55.240 --> 00:32:57.790
Specifically, it's a bad idea
to say that discrimination

00:32:57.790 --> 00:33:01.460
is wrong just because the traits
of all humans are identical.

00:33:01.460 --> 00:33:03.880
It's a bad idea to say
that war, violence, rape,

00:33:03.880 --> 00:33:05.890
and greed are wrong
because humans are not

00:33:05.890 --> 00:33:08.170
naturally inclined to them.

00:33:08.170 --> 00:33:09.760
And it's a bad idea
to say that people

00:33:09.760 --> 00:33:11.500
are responsible
for their actions

00:33:11.500 --> 00:33:15.100
just because the causes of
those actions are mysterious.

00:33:15.100 --> 00:33:17.530
They're bad ideas
because they imply,

00:33:17.530 --> 00:33:19.540
either that scholars
must be prepared

00:33:19.540 --> 00:33:22.630
to fudge their data for
a higher moral purpose,

00:33:22.630 --> 00:33:25.650
or we must all be prepared
to relinquish our values.

00:33:25.650 --> 00:33:28.720
And I would argue that
that is one choice that I

00:33:28.720 --> 00:33:30.190
hope we don't have to make.

00:33:30.190 --> 00:33:31.519
Thank you very much.

00:33:31.519 --> 00:33:35.012
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:42.496 --> 00:33:42.996
Thanks.

00:33:42.996 --> 00:33:44.493
KEYSER: That was great, Steve.

00:33:44.493 --> 00:33:45.491
PINKER: Thanks, Jay.

00:33:48.490 --> 00:33:50.920
KEYSER: Our next speaker
is Hilary Putnam.

00:33:53.800 --> 00:33:58.790
I first met Hilary when
I came to MIT in 1961.

00:33:58.790 --> 00:34:02.980
It was his first of five
years on the MIT faculty

00:34:02.980 --> 00:34:08.030
before moving on to Harvard,
where he's been ever since.

00:34:08.030 --> 00:34:11.010
In a review of
Hilary's recent book,

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:14.360
The Threefold Cord,
Mind, Body, and World,

00:34:14.360 --> 00:34:17.810
his fellow philosopher
Simon Blackburn says,

00:34:17.810 --> 00:34:21.620
quote, "Hilary Putnam is one of
the most distinguished living

00:34:21.620 --> 00:34:24.320
philosophers whose brilliant
and fertile writings now

00:34:24.320 --> 00:34:26.429
span half a century.

00:34:26.429 --> 00:34:28.679
His range is enormous.

00:34:28.679 --> 00:34:31.010
It is difficult to think
of an aspect of philosophy

00:34:31.010 --> 00:34:33.560
that he has not touched,
from formal logic

00:34:33.560 --> 00:34:37.100
to the philosophy of religion,
from quantum theory to ethics.

00:34:37.100 --> 00:34:38.840
He has been constantly
at the center

00:34:38.840 --> 00:34:41.900
of debates in the philosophy
of mind and language,

00:34:41.900 --> 00:34:44.150
where positions that he
was the first to articulate

00:34:44.150 --> 00:34:46.489
have become landmarks
in the field."

00:34:46.489 --> 00:34:48.739
End quote.

00:34:48.739 --> 00:34:50.750
A colleague of mine
recently told me

00:34:50.750 --> 00:34:54.830
that when the list of great
philosophers of the last 50

00:34:54.830 --> 00:34:59.480
years is compiled, Hilary
Putnam will be among them.

00:34:59.480 --> 00:35:01.190
We are fortunate
to have him here

00:35:01.190 --> 00:35:05.440
to help us celebrate 50
years of humanities at MIT.

00:35:05.440 --> 00:35:06.586
Hilary Putnam.

00:35:06.586 --> 00:35:09.918
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:17.480 --> 00:35:18.980
PUTNAM: I'm going
to put these here.

00:35:28.970 --> 00:35:31.610
Compared to Steve
Pinker, my range tonight

00:35:31.610 --> 00:35:32.525
will not be enormous.

00:35:35.150 --> 00:35:39.140
In the Encyclopedia
of Philosophy,

00:35:39.140 --> 00:35:42.020
the article titled
Godel's Theorem

00:35:42.020 --> 00:35:46.035
begins with a terse statement.

00:35:53.335 --> 00:35:54.210
Why is it doing that?

00:36:02.660 --> 00:36:05.840
This is a disaster.

00:36:05.840 --> 00:36:07.870
Any idea?

00:36:07.870 --> 00:36:09.080
Something about the slide.

00:36:09.080 --> 00:36:10.370
I don't know.

00:36:10.370 --> 00:36:16.450
Well, this unreadable--
this opaque transparency.

00:36:19.583 --> 00:36:22.000
I think the lady who made it
didn't know how to make them.

00:36:22.000 --> 00:36:23.380
Begins with a following
terse statement.

00:36:23.380 --> 00:36:26.020
Those of you who knew him may--
in the interest know it was

00:36:26.020 --> 00:36:29.350
written by Jonathan
[? Huyenard. ?] By

00:36:29.350 --> 00:36:36.850
Godel's Theorem, the following
statement is generally meant,

00:36:36.850 --> 00:36:40.920
in any formal system
adequate for number theory--

00:36:40.920 --> 00:36:43.990
no-- we just had it.

00:36:43.990 --> 00:36:45.430
Well, it's a dark subject.

00:36:50.230 --> 00:36:51.550
In any formal system--

00:36:51.550 --> 00:36:53.050
and this is the
first time I haven't

00:36:53.050 --> 00:36:55.810
made my own transparencies.

00:36:55.810 --> 00:37:00.000
From now on I go back
to making my own.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:02.640
There exists an
undesirable formula.

00:37:02.640 --> 00:37:04.940
There is a formula
that is not provable,

00:37:04.940 --> 00:37:07.240
and whose negation
is not provable.

00:37:07.240 --> 00:37:09.070
This statement is
occasionally referred to

00:37:09.070 --> 00:37:12.020
as Godel's first theorem.

00:37:12.020 --> 00:37:15.670
A corollary to the theorem
is that the consistency

00:37:15.670 --> 00:37:19.390
of a formal system adequate for
number theory cannot be proved

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:20.920
within the system.

00:37:20.920 --> 00:37:23.050
Sometime it is this
corollary that is

00:37:23.050 --> 00:37:25.120
referred to as Godel's theorem.

00:37:25.120 --> 00:37:28.780
It is also referred to as
Godel's second theorem.

00:37:28.780 --> 00:37:30.640
These statements
are somewhat vaguely

00:37:30.640 --> 00:37:34.900
formulated generalizations
of results published in 1931

00:37:34.900 --> 00:37:38.590
by Kurt Godel then in Vienna.

00:37:38.590 --> 00:37:41.080
In spite of the
forbidding technicality

00:37:41.080 --> 00:37:44.590
of Godel's original
paper, the Godel theorem

00:37:44.590 --> 00:37:48.130
has never stopped generating
enormous interest.

00:37:48.130 --> 00:37:50.770
Much of that interest
is aroused by the fact

00:37:50.770 --> 00:37:53.440
that with the proof
of the Godel theorem,

00:37:53.440 --> 00:37:55.870
the human mind
succeeded in proving

00:37:55.870 --> 00:37:57.880
that there was something
it could not prove,

00:37:57.880 --> 00:38:02.110
or at least not in any
fixed consistent system

00:38:02.110 --> 00:38:05.050
with any fixed
finite set of axioms

00:38:05.050 --> 00:38:07.360
and with the usual
logic as the instrument

00:38:07.360 --> 00:38:13.120
with which deductions are to
be made from those axioms.

00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:15.730
In fact, the theorem is
much stronger than this.

00:38:15.730 --> 00:38:18.070
Even if we allow the
system in question

00:38:18.070 --> 00:38:21.190
to contain an infinite
list of axioms

00:38:21.190 --> 00:38:25.150
and to have an infinite list of
additional rules of inference,

00:38:25.150 --> 00:38:27.610
the theorem still applies.

00:38:27.610 --> 00:38:29.770
Provided, the two
lists can be generated

00:38:29.770 --> 00:38:33.790
by a computer, which is allowed
to go on running forever.

00:38:33.790 --> 00:38:37.030
Moreover, instead of
speaking a formal systems

00:38:37.030 --> 00:38:40.960
adequate for number theory, or
adequate for at least number

00:38:40.960 --> 00:38:45.190
theory, one can
speak of computers.

00:38:45.190 --> 00:38:49.420
Such version was first
stated by Alan Turing.

00:38:49.420 --> 00:38:51.670
The theorem says that
if a computer is allowed

00:38:51.670 --> 00:38:54.100
to write down formulas
of number theory

00:38:54.100 --> 00:38:57.070
forever, subject
to the constraints

00:38:57.070 --> 00:39:01.540
that, one, any deductive
consequence of finite

00:39:01.540 --> 00:39:05.680
be many of the formulas in
the list that it writes down

00:39:05.680 --> 00:39:08.740
will also get written down
in the list sooner or later,

00:39:08.740 --> 00:39:12.100
and, two, the list
is not inconsistent,

00:39:12.100 --> 00:39:15.160
that is it doesn't contain
some formula and the negation

00:39:15.160 --> 00:39:19.310
of that same formula,
then there is a formula,

00:39:19.310 --> 00:39:22.450
in fact, the formula that says
that the list is consistent

00:39:22.450 --> 00:39:25.180
is an example, that
is not included

00:39:25.180 --> 00:39:29.050
in the list of formulas
generated by the computer.

00:39:29.050 --> 00:39:31.750
If we speak of the formulas
listed by the computer

00:39:31.750 --> 00:39:37.120
as proved by the computer, and
of a computer of the kind just

00:39:37.120 --> 00:39:40.240
mentioned as a
consistent computer,

00:39:40.240 --> 00:39:42.430
we can say this
succinctly as follows.

00:39:50.236 --> 00:39:52.290
Yeah, this will be another one.

00:39:55.640 --> 00:39:56.140
No, no.

00:39:56.140 --> 00:39:56.932
This is the wrong--

00:40:09.530 --> 00:40:10.760
oh, I guess I did--

00:40:10.760 --> 00:40:12.520
did I?

00:40:12.520 --> 00:40:14.270
Oh, yes, it was at the
bottom of the page.

00:40:14.270 --> 00:40:17.430
Yes, the bottom
of the same page.

00:40:17.430 --> 00:40:19.460
That's right.

00:40:19.460 --> 00:40:28.040
There is a procedure by which,
given any consistent computer,

00:40:28.040 --> 00:40:31.970
a human mathematician can write
down a formula of number theory

00:40:31.970 --> 00:40:34.760
that the computer cannot prove.

00:40:34.760 --> 00:40:38.120
Of course, those who draw
remarkable conclusions from

00:40:38.120 --> 00:40:40.550
this don't mention you could
also say there's a procedure

00:40:40.550 --> 00:40:43.790
by which, given any
consistent computer,

00:40:43.790 --> 00:40:47.480
a computer can write down a
formula of number theory that

00:40:47.480 --> 00:40:50.090
the computer cannot prove.

00:40:50.090 --> 00:40:54.590
In 1961, the Oxford philosopher
John Lucas claimed that

00:40:54.590 --> 00:40:59.398
the Godel's theorem shows that
non-computational processes,

00:40:59.398 --> 00:41:01.910
processes that cannot in
principle be carried out

00:41:01.910 --> 00:41:05.900
by a digital computer, even if
its memory space is unlimited,

00:41:05.900 --> 00:41:08.300
go on in our minds.

00:41:08.300 --> 00:41:10.760
He concluded that our minds
cannot be identical with

00:41:10.760 --> 00:41:13.640
our brains, or with
any material system,

00:41:13.640 --> 00:41:17.630
since assuming standard physics,
the latter cannot carry out

00:41:17.630 --> 00:41:19.550
non-computational processes.

00:41:19.550 --> 00:41:21.790
Actually, that's not
actually known, by the way,

00:41:21.790 --> 00:41:23.870
but I won't jump up
and down on that.

00:41:23.870 --> 00:41:26.930
And hence, that our
minds are immaterial.

00:41:26.930 --> 00:41:30.410
More recently, Roger Penrose
used a similar but more

00:41:30.410 --> 00:41:32.870
elaborate argument
to claim that we

00:41:32.870 --> 00:41:35.660
need to make fundamental
changes in the way we

00:41:35.660 --> 00:41:38.840
view both the mind and
the physical world.

00:41:38.840 --> 00:41:41.120
Penrose, too, claims
that the Godel's theorem

00:41:41.120 --> 00:41:44.540
shows that non-computational
processes must go on

00:41:44.540 --> 00:41:46.490
in the human mind.

00:41:46.490 --> 00:41:49.370
But instead of positing an
immaterial soul for them

00:41:49.370 --> 00:41:53.030
to go on in, he concludes
that these non-computational

00:41:53.030 --> 00:41:56.540
processes must be
physical processes.

00:41:56.540 --> 00:42:00.140
Physical processes in the
brain, and our physics

00:42:00.140 --> 00:42:03.440
must change to account for them.

00:42:03.440 --> 00:42:07.600
I was early called upon to
review Penrose's argument.

00:42:07.600 --> 00:42:09.410
And my review was
later republished

00:42:09.410 --> 00:42:12.110
in the American Math Society.

00:42:12.110 --> 00:42:13.760
And like the others
who studied it,

00:42:13.760 --> 00:42:17.030
I found many unfillable gaps.

00:42:17.030 --> 00:42:19.160
But relax, I'm not
going to ask you

00:42:19.160 --> 00:42:22.300
to listen to the details
of Penrose's argument,

00:42:22.300 --> 00:42:25.460
nor to a listing of the
places at which it goes wrong.

00:42:25.460 --> 00:42:27.500
That's something you can
find in the literature,

00:42:27.500 --> 00:42:29.660
if you are interested.

00:42:29.660 --> 00:42:31.880
The fact is that
Penrose himself seems

00:42:31.880 --> 00:42:34.580
to recognize that there
are gaps in his argument,

00:42:34.580 --> 00:42:37.080
even though at the beginning
he promises, I quote,

00:42:37.080 --> 00:42:39.770
"clear and simple
proof," unquotes.

00:42:39.770 --> 00:42:41.540
And in the end,
he offers a series

00:42:41.540 --> 00:42:43.670
of arguments, which
are not mathematical

00:42:43.670 --> 00:42:46.060
at all but philosophical.

00:42:46.060 --> 00:42:49.330
The philosophical arguments
that Penrose throws in

00:42:49.330 --> 00:42:51.860
to plug the gap in his
mathematical reasoning

00:42:51.860 --> 00:42:53.120
are numerous.

00:42:53.120 --> 00:42:56.330
And they're not sharply set out,
but the heart of his reasoning

00:42:56.330 --> 00:42:58.500
seems to go as follows.

00:42:58.500 --> 00:43:01.370
Let us suppose that the
brain of an ideally competent

00:43:01.370 --> 00:43:06.670
mathematician can be represented
by a consistent computer,

00:43:06.670 --> 00:43:09.800
or a consistent computer
program, a program which

00:43:09.800 --> 00:43:14.150
generates all and only
correct and convincing proofs.

00:43:14.150 --> 00:43:16.250
The statement that the
program is consistent

00:43:16.250 --> 00:43:20.530
is not one that the program
generates by Godel's theorem.

00:43:20.530 --> 00:43:22.820
So if an ideal
human mathematician

00:43:22.820 --> 00:43:28.380
could prove that statement,
we would have a contradiction.

00:43:28.380 --> 00:43:33.980
This part of Penrose's reasoning
is uncontroversial correct.

00:43:33.980 --> 00:43:37.723
It is possible, however,
that the program--

00:43:37.723 --> 00:43:39.140
remember, this
program is supposed

00:43:39.140 --> 00:43:46.500
to reproduce the competence of
an ideal human mathematician.

00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:49.440
It's possible, however,
that this supposed program

00:43:49.440 --> 00:43:53.370
is too long to be consciously
apprehended at all, as long

00:43:53.370 --> 00:43:56.760
as the Boston telephone book,
or even orders of magnitude

00:43:56.760 --> 00:43:58.260
longer.

00:43:58.260 --> 00:44:02.820
In that case, Penrose
asks, how could evolution

00:44:02.820 --> 00:44:05.870
have produced such a program?

00:44:05.870 --> 00:44:07.720
It would have to
have evolved in parts

00:44:07.720 --> 00:44:09.790
and there would be no
evolutionary advantage

00:44:09.790 --> 00:44:11.710
to the parts.

00:44:11.710 --> 00:44:14.620
But the objection that it's hard
to see how such a thing could

00:44:14.620 --> 00:44:18.460
evolve in parts is a
standard objection to all,

00:44:18.460 --> 00:44:22.370
or just about all
evolutionary explanations.

00:44:22.370 --> 00:44:25.840
One, that it has proved possible
to overcome in case after case,

00:44:25.840 --> 00:44:28.000
so though the details
cannot, of course,

00:44:28.000 --> 00:44:31.130
be predicted in advance.

00:44:31.130 --> 00:44:34.390
First of all, evolution
had better not endow

00:44:34.390 --> 00:44:38.410
intelligent beings who are to
have any chance of survival

00:44:38.410 --> 00:44:41.230
with reasoning patterns
that lead to contradictions

00:44:41.230 --> 00:44:43.420
in daily practice.

00:44:43.420 --> 00:44:45.610
If we were always falling
into contradiction

00:44:45.610 --> 00:44:47.230
in our everyday
reasoning, we would

00:44:47.230 --> 00:44:50.080
be so confused we would not
be around long enough to pass

00:44:50.080 --> 00:44:52.990
on our genes.

00:44:52.990 --> 00:44:55.090
Of course, there are parts
of mathematics in which

00:44:55.090 --> 00:44:57.940
contradictions have arisen.

00:44:57.940 --> 00:45:00.700
For example, in Cantor's
theory of transfinite numbers.

00:45:00.700 --> 00:45:03.940
It was at first possible to
prove both that there was

00:45:03.940 --> 00:45:08.170
and that there was not a
greatest transfinite ordinal.

00:45:08.170 --> 00:45:11.170
What mathematicians did was
to add to Cantor's theory

00:45:11.170 --> 00:45:13.420
certain, more or less,
ad hoc restrictions,

00:45:13.420 --> 00:45:16.720
such as Russell's
theory of types

00:45:16.720 --> 00:45:18.730
to get rid of the paradox,
or to get rid of it

00:45:18.730 --> 00:45:20.760
as far as we know.

00:45:20.760 --> 00:45:23.860
In short, everyday mathematical
reasoning is consistent.

00:45:23.860 --> 00:45:26.240
It wouldn't have
arisen if it wasn't.

00:45:26.240 --> 00:45:28.420
And when [? Rishashay ?]
mathematics

00:45:28.420 --> 00:45:30.250
turns out to contain
contradictions,

00:45:30.250 --> 00:45:32.620
we simply troubleshoot it.

00:45:32.620 --> 00:45:34.240
In fact, even before
Cantor, we had

00:45:34.240 --> 00:45:38.140
to troubleshoot the definitions
of continuity, limits,

00:45:38.140 --> 00:45:40.420
et cetera, in the calculus.

00:45:40.420 --> 00:45:42.700
The consistency of
mathematics to the extent

00:45:42.700 --> 00:45:46.990
that it is consistent
is not such a miracle.

00:45:46.990 --> 00:45:49.480
I think I know Penrose's
response to this argument.

00:45:49.480 --> 00:45:50.980
In fact, he has a
website in which

00:45:50.980 --> 00:45:55.840
he gives responses to my
criticisms and those of others.

00:45:55.840 --> 00:45:59.350
He will say I believe that
evolution might explain

00:45:59.350 --> 00:46:02.710
why we evolved a program
which is consistent,

00:46:02.710 --> 00:46:05.030
at least in its
elementary parts,

00:46:05.030 --> 00:46:07.210
such as the theory
of whole numbers,

00:46:07.210 --> 00:46:10.820
but that we evolved a style of
reasoning, which is correct,

00:46:10.820 --> 00:46:13.840
that is which corresponds
to the platonic truth

00:46:13.840 --> 00:46:17.500
about the numbers,
would be a miracle.

00:46:17.500 --> 00:46:21.490
The alternative, he suggests, is
that instead of a huge program,

00:46:21.490 --> 00:46:24.670
which miraculously corresponds
to the platonic truth

00:46:24.670 --> 00:46:27.070
about the numbers, we have
something quite different,

00:46:27.070 --> 00:46:29.950
a purely physical
capacity to perform

00:46:29.950 --> 00:46:34.910
non-recursive operations
in our brains.

00:46:34.910 --> 00:46:36.590
Of course, this
argument of Penrose

00:46:36.590 --> 00:46:38.150
is one to appeal
only to those who

00:46:38.150 --> 00:46:41.260
are willing to accept his
odd combination of views,

00:46:41.260 --> 00:46:44.060
a Platonist view of
mathematics, and a purely

00:46:44.060 --> 00:46:47.630
materialist view of the brain.

00:46:47.630 --> 00:46:50.150
What his argument
ignores is the fact

00:46:50.150 --> 00:46:53.690
that the correct interpretation
of our mathematical concepts

00:46:53.690 --> 00:46:57.410
depends on the use
we make of them.

00:46:57.410 --> 00:46:59.540
Evolution might indeed
have programmed us

00:46:59.540 --> 00:47:02.900
with a different formal
system, but to imagine

00:47:02.900 --> 00:47:05.600
that we had been endowed by
evolution with a radically

00:47:05.600 --> 00:47:07.790
different system is
the same as imagining

00:47:07.790 --> 00:47:09.980
that we had been endowed
with a disposition

00:47:09.980 --> 00:47:12.990
to develop different concepts.

00:47:12.990 --> 00:47:14.780
It's not as if the
meanings of our words

00:47:14.780 --> 00:47:17.800
were fixed prior to
what we do with them,

00:47:17.800 --> 00:47:20.180
and evolution had the
task of endowing us

00:47:20.180 --> 00:47:24.590
with a program for using words
with those antecedently-fixed

00:47:24.590 --> 00:47:25.820
meanings.

00:47:25.820 --> 00:47:28.670
Evolution just has
to enable us, it just

00:47:28.670 --> 00:47:30.620
has to give us a program
which will enable

00:47:30.620 --> 00:47:33.230
us to succeed in our lives.

00:47:33.230 --> 00:47:35.300
If we do, then our
mathematical concepts

00:47:35.300 --> 00:47:38.660
will admit of some
interpretation, under which

00:47:38.660 --> 00:47:40.680
what we say is right.

00:47:40.680 --> 00:47:43.400
In fact, that follows from
another theorem of Godel,

00:47:43.400 --> 00:47:46.250
the so-called Godel's
completeness theorem.

00:47:46.250 --> 00:47:49.430
To see what we can learn
about the human mind,

00:47:49.430 --> 00:47:51.770
or at least about how we
should or rather shouldn't

00:47:51.770 --> 00:47:54.740
think about the human mind
from the Godel theorem,

00:47:54.740 --> 00:47:58.280
I need to point out two
objections that must be raised

00:47:58.280 --> 00:48:01.130
against the very question
that Penrose asks,

00:48:01.130 --> 00:48:03.170
the question whether
the set of theorems

00:48:03.170 --> 00:48:05.870
that an ideal
mathematician can prove

00:48:05.870 --> 00:48:07.550
could be generated
by a computer.

00:48:19.420 --> 00:48:21.050
I don't have a slide
to go with this.

00:48:29.790 --> 00:48:33.660
The first objection, and this
is a point I want to emphasize,

00:48:33.660 --> 00:48:36.930
is that the notion of
simulating the performance

00:48:36.930 --> 00:48:40.110
of a mathematician
is highly unclear,

00:48:40.110 --> 00:48:43.500
or perhaps, I should
say, highly ambiguous.

00:48:43.500 --> 00:48:45.000
Perhaps, the
question whether it's

00:48:45.000 --> 00:48:47.460
possible to build a
machine that behaves

00:48:47.460 --> 00:48:50.700
as a typical human
mathematician behaves

00:48:50.700 --> 00:48:54.300
is a meaningful
empirical question.

00:48:54.300 --> 00:48:57.150
Say, can you build a machine
which passes a Turing

00:48:57.150 --> 00:49:00.850
test for being a mathematician?

00:49:00.850 --> 00:49:04.740
But a typical human
mathematician makes mistakes.

00:49:04.740 --> 00:49:06.690
The output of an
actual mathematician

00:49:06.690 --> 00:49:09.360
contains inconsistencies,
especially

00:49:09.360 --> 00:49:11.910
if we are to imagine that
she goes on proving theorems

00:49:11.910 --> 00:49:16.810
forever, as the application
of the Godel theorem requires.

00:49:16.810 --> 00:49:19.260
So the question of proving
the whole of this output

00:49:19.260 --> 00:49:22.680
is consistent does
not even arise.

00:49:22.680 --> 00:49:28.380
To this, Penrose replies in the
Shadows of the Mind, his book

00:49:28.380 --> 00:49:31.100
Shadows of the Mind, that the
mathematician may make errors,

00:49:31.100 --> 00:49:34.180
but she corrects
them upon reflection.

00:49:34.180 --> 00:49:38.040
This is true, but to simulate
mathematicians who sometimes

00:49:38.040 --> 00:49:40.620
change their minds about
what they've proved,

00:49:40.620 --> 00:49:42.540
we would need a
program, which is also

00:49:42.540 --> 00:49:44.220
allowed to change its mind.

00:49:44.220 --> 00:49:46.320
There are such programs,
but Godel's theorem

00:49:46.320 --> 00:49:48.630
does not apply to them.

00:49:48.630 --> 00:49:51.120
Trial and error machines,
they are called.

00:49:51.120 --> 00:49:54.000
The second objection is that
to confuse these questions,

00:49:54.000 --> 00:49:59.560
the question of what an
actual mathematician can do,

00:49:59.560 --> 00:50:01.920
and the question of what
an ideal mathematician who

00:50:01.920 --> 00:50:07.200
lives forever can do, is to
miss the normativity, the value

00:50:07.200 --> 00:50:11.790
laden character of the
notion of ideal mathematics.

00:50:11.790 --> 00:50:15.270
The description of
normatively ideal practice

00:50:15.270 --> 00:50:17.460
in mathematics,
or any other area,

00:50:17.460 --> 00:50:19.035
is not a problem for physics.

00:50:22.300 --> 00:50:24.970
It's not my aim
to criticize those

00:50:24.970 --> 00:50:27.580
who make the mistake of
thinking that the Godel theorem,

00:50:27.580 --> 00:50:30.610
or merely to criticize those
who make the mistake of thinking

00:50:30.610 --> 00:50:33.850
that the Godel theorem proves
that the human mind or brain

00:50:33.850 --> 00:50:37.510
can carry out
non-computational processes,

00:50:37.510 --> 00:50:40.060
that Lucas and Penrose
have made a serious blunder

00:50:40.060 --> 00:50:43.180
in their claims about what the
Godel theorem, quote, "shows

00:50:43.180 --> 00:50:46.390
about the human mind,"
unquote, is after all widely

00:50:46.390 --> 00:50:50.230
recognized, at least in
the logical community.

00:50:50.230 --> 00:50:52.750
At the same time,
however, a view

00:50:52.750 --> 00:50:55.330
which may seem just
at the opposite end

00:50:55.330 --> 00:50:59.590
of the philosophical spectrum
from the Lucas, Penrose view

00:50:59.590 --> 00:51:03.880
seems to me to be vulnerable
to the objections I just made.

00:51:03.880 --> 00:51:08.680
I refer to the widespread use
of the argument in philosophy

00:51:08.680 --> 00:51:12.910
as well as in cognitive science
that whenever human beings

00:51:12.910 --> 00:51:15.080
are able to recognize--

00:51:15.080 --> 00:51:16.465
here I've put this slide--

00:51:28.800 --> 00:51:33.280
I think she printed
this out on a notebook

00:51:33.280 --> 00:51:38.620
insert, or something, instead
of transparency paper.

00:51:38.620 --> 00:51:41.780
I refer to the--

00:51:41.780 --> 00:51:43.060
why can't we see it at all.

00:51:43.060 --> 00:51:44.185
Well, maybe when I read it.

00:51:44.185 --> 00:51:46.960
I refer to the widespread
use of the following

00:51:46.960 --> 00:51:49.030
principle in
philosophy as well as

00:51:49.030 --> 00:51:52.330
in cognitive science,
whenever human beings are

00:51:52.330 --> 00:51:55.330
able to recognize that
a property applies

00:51:55.330 --> 00:51:58.870
to a sentence in a,
quote, "potentially

00:51:58.870 --> 00:52:01.240
infinite," unquote,
IE, a fantastically

00:52:01.240 --> 00:52:04.000
large set of cases,
then there must

00:52:04.000 --> 00:52:07.630
be an algorithm in their
brains that accounts for this,

00:52:07.630 --> 00:52:09.820
and the task of
cognitive science

00:52:09.820 --> 00:52:13.300
must be to describe
that algorithm.

00:52:13.300 --> 00:52:17.500
The case of mathematics shows
that that cannot be a universal

00:52:17.500 --> 00:52:19.000
truth.

00:52:19.000 --> 00:52:21.580
And my criticism of
Penrose can enable

00:52:21.580 --> 00:52:25.600
us to see why we shouldn't
draw any mystical conclusions

00:52:25.600 --> 00:52:28.960
about either the mind or
the brain from the fact

00:52:28.960 --> 00:52:32.500
that it isn't a universal truth.

00:52:32.500 --> 00:52:36.550
But first, a terribly
important qualification,

00:52:36.550 --> 00:52:40.030
especially given who my
fellow symposiists are.

00:52:40.030 --> 00:52:43.870
And Noam and I were
undergraduates in the program

00:52:43.870 --> 00:52:46.240
in linguistics at the
University of Pennsylvania

00:52:46.240 --> 00:52:51.460
together as far back as 1944.

00:52:51.460 --> 00:52:54.040
I have no doubt that human
beings have the ability

00:52:54.040 --> 00:52:57.980
to employ recursions,
another name for algorithms,

00:52:57.980 --> 00:53:00.370
both consciously
and unconsciously,

00:53:00.370 --> 00:53:04.350
and that there are
recognition out abilities

00:53:04.350 --> 00:53:07.670
that cognitive science should
explain, and in many cases,

00:53:07.670 --> 00:53:12.640
does explain by appealing to
the notion of an algorithm.

00:53:12.640 --> 00:53:15.250
The magnificent contribution
of Noam Chomsky,

00:53:15.250 --> 00:53:18.130
in particular, shows
our ability to recognize

00:53:18.130 --> 00:53:23.470
the property of grammaticality
is explainable in this way.

00:53:23.470 --> 00:53:26.860
Why then do I say that the case
of mathematics shows that it

00:53:26.860 --> 00:53:30.970
cannot be a universal truth,
that whenever human beings are

00:53:30.970 --> 00:53:34.090
able to recognize that a
property applies to a sentence

00:53:34.090 --> 00:53:37.870
in a potentially infinite, that
is a fantastically large set

00:53:37.870 --> 00:53:41.500
of cases, there must be an
algorithm in their brains that

00:53:41.500 --> 00:53:42.520
accounts for it?

00:53:50.612 --> 00:53:52.390
Not quite ready for this.

00:53:59.320 --> 00:54:03.060
Well, just as it is true that
we can recognize grammaticality

00:54:03.060 --> 00:54:06.040
in a fantastically
large set of cases,

00:54:06.040 --> 00:54:11.130
it is true that we can recognize
being proved in a fantastically

00:54:11.130 --> 00:54:13.410
large set of cases.

00:54:13.410 --> 00:54:15.840
And the Godel theorem does
show, although, by the way,

00:54:15.840 --> 00:54:19.860
this requires a different
argument from Penrose,

00:54:19.860 --> 00:54:23.700
that if there is an algorithm
that accounts for this ability,

00:54:23.700 --> 00:54:26.820
we're not going to be able
to verify that there is.

00:54:26.820 --> 00:54:31.500
That is the Godel theorem does
not show that our cognitive

00:54:31.500 --> 00:54:36.180
competence cannot be totally
represented by a program,

00:54:36.180 --> 00:54:41.220
but it does show, at least
if you extend the argument,

00:54:41.220 --> 00:54:43.410
and include not just on
deductive competence,

00:54:43.410 --> 00:54:49.500
but our inductive competence,
that if there is a program that

00:54:49.500 --> 00:54:55.050
simulates our ideal rationality,
then it would be beyond even

00:54:55.050 --> 00:54:59.790
our ideal rationality to
recognize the program that does

00:54:59.790 --> 00:55:00.945
it is having that property.

00:55:04.330 --> 00:55:05.880
But I think it would be wrong--

00:55:05.880 --> 00:55:07.380
and this is a
conclusion I point out

00:55:07.380 --> 00:55:10.810
in a paper called Reflexive
Reflection some years ago.

00:55:10.810 --> 00:55:14.400
But I think it's wrong to just
stop with that conclusion.

00:55:14.400 --> 00:55:17.430
It's wrong because talk about
a potentially infinite set

00:55:17.430 --> 00:55:21.240
of cases only makes sense when
there's an algorithm that we

00:55:21.240 --> 00:55:23.820
can verify.

00:55:23.820 --> 00:55:26.620
Why do I say this?

00:55:26.620 --> 00:55:29.160
Well, think for a minute
about the mathematician

00:55:29.160 --> 00:55:32.520
that Penrose imagines
he is talking about.

00:55:32.520 --> 00:55:34.470
If the mathematician
could really

00:55:34.470 --> 00:55:37.890
recognize the potential
infinity of theorems,

00:55:37.890 --> 00:55:40.080
then she would be able
to recognize theorems

00:55:40.080 --> 00:55:43.170
of arbitrary finite lengths.

00:55:43.170 --> 00:55:45.540
That's what it means
to be able to recognize

00:55:45.540 --> 00:55:47.790
a potential infinity
of theorems.

00:55:47.790 --> 00:55:51.660
For example, theorems to be
too long to be written down

00:55:51.660 --> 00:55:54.780
before all the stars are
cooled down, or sucked

00:55:54.780 --> 00:55:56.340
into black holes, or whatever.

00:55:59.230 --> 00:56:05.230
But any adequate physics of
the human brain, even the one

00:56:05.230 --> 00:56:09.760
proposed by Penrose, if
it turns out to be right,

00:56:09.760 --> 00:56:12.460
will certainly entail that
the brain will disintegrate

00:56:12.460 --> 00:56:13.960
long before it
gets through trying

00:56:13.960 --> 00:56:16.890
to prove any such theorem.

00:56:16.890 --> 00:56:22.080
In real actual fact, the set of
theorems, a physically possible

00:56:22.080 --> 00:56:26.100
human mathematician
can prove is finite.

00:56:26.100 --> 00:56:29.530
Moreover, it's not really a set.

00:56:29.530 --> 00:56:31.220
This is why I think
my little theorem

00:56:31.220 --> 00:56:36.290
about the unrecognized ability
of any algorithm that totally

00:56:36.290 --> 00:56:38.420
reproduced ideal human
competence, so it doesn't

00:56:38.420 --> 00:56:40.700
engage the real issues,
because it grants that there

00:56:40.700 --> 00:56:43.500
is such a thing as the set.

00:56:43.500 --> 00:56:50.180
I don't think there's-- it's
not a set, because sets,

00:56:50.180 --> 00:56:53.930
by definition, are too valued,
an item's either in or out.

00:56:53.930 --> 00:56:56.480
But the predicate
prove is vague.

00:56:56.480 --> 00:56:58.820
We can, of course, make
it precise by specifying

00:56:58.820 --> 00:57:02.360
a fixed list of axioms
and rules to be used.

00:57:02.360 --> 00:57:05.240
And that was why, in fact,
axiomatic mathematics

00:57:05.240 --> 00:57:06.980
was developed in
the 19th century,

00:57:06.980 --> 00:57:12.090
to make the notion of a proof
more precise, but at a cost.

00:57:12.090 --> 00:57:13.760
The Godel theorem
shows that when

00:57:13.760 --> 00:57:16.760
you make the notion of
approved precise in that way

00:57:16.760 --> 00:57:19.360
by fixing the axioms and
rules to be used, then

00:57:19.360 --> 00:57:21.260
there's a perfectly
natural sense in which we

00:57:21.260 --> 00:57:25.550
can prove a statement which is
not drivable from those axioms

00:57:25.550 --> 00:57:26.623
by those rules.

00:57:26.623 --> 00:57:28.040
In other words,
you didn't capture

00:57:28.040 --> 00:57:33.110
the whole of the notion of
informal mathematical proof.

00:57:33.110 --> 00:57:36.620
Now, you might be driven to
say, OK, by proof, I mean,

00:57:36.620 --> 00:57:40.550
or by prove, we mean
prove by any means we

00:57:40.550 --> 00:57:42.140
can see to be correct.

00:57:45.350 --> 00:57:47.870
But this uses the
essentially vague notion,

00:57:47.870 --> 00:57:50.210
see to be correct.

00:57:50.210 --> 00:57:52.940
In short, there isn't a
potentially infinite set

00:57:52.940 --> 00:57:55.610
of theorems a
mathematician can prove

00:57:55.610 --> 00:58:00.650
concerning which we can ask, is
it recursive, or non-recursive?

00:58:00.650 --> 00:58:04.490
What there is a vague finite
collection of theorems

00:58:04.490 --> 00:58:08.360
that a real flesh and blood
mathematician can prove.

00:58:08.360 --> 00:58:11.780
And vague finite collections
are neither recursive nor

00:58:11.780 --> 00:58:13.370
non-recursive.

00:58:13.370 --> 00:58:17.050
Those concepts only apply
to well-defined sets.

00:58:26.710 --> 00:58:27.210
Awful.

00:58:29.770 --> 00:58:31.150
But it may be objected.

00:58:31.150 --> 00:58:34.600
Didn't Chomsky teach us
that we can and should

00:58:34.600 --> 00:58:38.170
idealize language
users by imagining

00:58:38.170 --> 00:58:41.320
that, like ideal computers,
they can go on generating

00:58:41.320 --> 00:58:46.300
grammatical sentences forever,
sentences of arbitrary length?

00:58:46.300 --> 00:58:47.770
Yes, he did.

00:58:47.770 --> 00:58:50.560
But the idealization he
showed us how to make

00:58:50.560 --> 00:58:54.250
is only precise
because it corresponds

00:58:54.250 --> 00:58:57.190
to a well-defined algorithm.

00:58:57.190 --> 00:59:00.700
If we take the question, what
can an ideal mathematician

00:59:00.700 --> 00:59:02.740
prove to be a precise one?

00:59:02.740 --> 00:59:07.060
We must specify just how
the mathematicians all too

00:59:07.060 --> 00:59:11.200
finite brain is to be
idealized, just as Chomsky did.

00:59:11.200 --> 00:59:14.060
And if we do it by specifying
an algorithm, then, of course,

00:59:14.060 --> 00:59:15.670
the result will
be computational.

00:59:21.180 --> 00:59:24.600
If this is right, then we should
not allow ourselves to say,

00:59:24.600 --> 00:59:27.690
the set of statements that
a mathematician can prove

00:59:27.690 --> 00:59:30.210
is a potentially infinite set.

00:59:30.210 --> 00:59:32.460
We should only say that
there are fantastically

00:59:32.460 --> 00:59:35.640
many statements a mathematician,
an actual mathematician

00:59:35.640 --> 00:59:38.850
can prove, at least in
this informal sense,

00:59:38.850 --> 00:59:40.920
and let it go with that.

00:59:40.920 --> 00:59:43.770
It does not follow
from that fact

00:59:43.770 --> 00:59:45.870
that there's an algorithm
such that everything

00:59:45.870 --> 00:59:49.800
an ideally competent
mathematician would, or should

00:59:49.800 --> 00:59:51.180
one say, should?

00:59:51.180 --> 00:59:53.580
Count as proving a
mathematical statement,

00:59:53.580 --> 00:59:55.535
is an instance of
that algorithm.

00:59:59.400 --> 00:59:59.900
Let's see.

00:59:59.900 --> 01:00:01.950
Do I have one more?

01:00:01.950 --> 01:00:02.540
Maybe so.

01:00:02.540 --> 01:00:04.040
Yeah, I guess that's it.

01:00:08.360 --> 01:00:09.320
Yeah, I don't have.

01:00:14.120 --> 01:00:15.530
It doesn't follow.

01:00:15.530 --> 01:00:18.810
As I say, that there is an
algorithm such that everything

01:00:18.810 --> 01:00:22.500
an ideally competent
mathematician would count,

01:00:22.500 --> 01:00:25.380
or should, count as proving
a mathematical statement is

01:00:25.380 --> 01:00:28.680
an instance of that algorithm,
any more than it follows

01:00:28.680 --> 01:00:30.900
from the fact that there
are fantastically many

01:00:30.900 --> 01:00:33.990
jokes that a person with
a good sense of humor

01:00:33.990 --> 01:00:35.730
can see to be funny.

01:00:35.730 --> 01:00:38.040
That there's an algorithm,
such that everything

01:00:38.040 --> 01:00:41.640
a person with an ideal sense
of humor would see to be funny

01:00:41.640 --> 01:00:44.310
is an instance of
that algorithm.

01:00:44.310 --> 01:00:45.900
There is a moral
here for all who

01:00:45.900 --> 01:00:47.990
are interested in
cognitive science.

01:00:47.990 --> 01:00:49.154
Thank you.

01:00:49.154 --> 01:00:52.598
[APPLAUSE]

01:01:16.750 --> 01:01:19.450
KEYSER: When I came
to MIT 39 years ago,

01:01:19.450 --> 01:01:22.420
I had been invited
to join a project

01:01:22.420 --> 01:01:26.920
under the auspices of Morris
Halle and Noam Chomsky.

01:01:26.920 --> 01:01:28.900
Morris was at the Center
for Advanced Study

01:01:28.900 --> 01:01:32.710
in the Behavioral Sciences
at Palo Alto when I came.

01:01:32.710 --> 01:01:36.610
So Noam was the first
person I met at MIT.

01:01:36.610 --> 01:01:38.677
I took him to be
typical of the place,

01:01:38.677 --> 01:01:39.760
and so I thought I'd come.

01:01:46.970 --> 01:01:50.390
At the time, there
was what Tom Kuhn

01:01:50.390 --> 01:01:53.738
would have called a paradigm
shift in linguistics.

01:01:53.738 --> 01:01:55.280
The scholars under
whom I had studied

01:01:55.280 --> 01:01:58.760
did things one way and
Noam did it another.

01:01:58.760 --> 01:02:02.600
The discussions were unusually
heated and not without cause.

01:02:02.600 --> 01:02:05.420
If Noam was right, then
much of the work being

01:02:05.420 --> 01:02:07.820
done in the field
was not just wrong,

01:02:07.820 --> 01:02:14.180
which in science is no crime,
but worse, it was useless.

01:02:14.180 --> 01:02:17.270
I remember walking along
Vassar Street with Noam

01:02:17.270 --> 01:02:21.680
to a coffee shop on Mass Avenue
where the student center now

01:02:21.680 --> 01:02:23.120
sits.

01:02:23.120 --> 01:02:25.280
I regret that many
people in this audience

01:02:25.280 --> 01:02:27.660
can't have that
memory in their heads.

01:02:27.660 --> 01:02:29.840
It was a wonderful
shopfront there

01:02:29.840 --> 01:02:32.780
and there was a coffee house,
like the Balt in Princeton,

01:02:32.780 --> 01:02:35.970
and we used to go there
and it was a great place.

01:02:35.970 --> 01:02:38.840
I think WBUR had
radio station there.

01:02:38.840 --> 01:02:43.890
And it all changed
when there was a fire.

01:02:43.890 --> 01:02:46.680
Anyway, I was walking with
Noam to that coffee shop,

01:02:46.680 --> 01:02:51.630
and I asked him what his opinion
of the linguistic wars was.

01:02:51.630 --> 01:02:56.220
He said, graduate students
have no vested interest.

01:02:56.220 --> 01:02:59.490
They will follow the most
interesting questions.

01:02:59.490 --> 01:03:04.070
He was right, they did,
and I was one of them.

01:03:04.070 --> 01:03:08.330
Later, Noam told me that, in his
opinion, success in linguistics

01:03:08.330 --> 01:03:13.250
was not a question of talent,
but rather of character,

01:03:13.250 --> 01:03:15.638
something which undoubtedly
explains the differences

01:03:15.638 --> 01:03:16.430
in our two careers.

01:03:24.640 --> 01:03:28.690
In 1974, I had the pleasure of
introducing Noam at the Summer

01:03:28.690 --> 01:03:31.390
Institute of Linguistics at
the University of Massachusetts

01:03:31.390 --> 01:03:33.190
at Amherst.

01:03:33.190 --> 01:03:37.300
Since everyone knew who he
was, I thought rather than read

01:03:37.300 --> 01:03:40.270
bits and pieces
from his biography,

01:03:40.270 --> 01:03:41.500
I would read his horoscope.

01:03:44.140 --> 01:03:46.580
25 years later, for
the same reason,

01:03:46.580 --> 01:03:51.200
I have the pleasure of
reading his horoscope again.

01:03:51.200 --> 01:04:00.190
Shake off lethargy, put
aside preconceived notions,

01:04:00.190 --> 01:04:06.205
emphasize the new, be
bold, in your presentation.

01:04:06.205 --> 01:04:07.666
Noam Chomsky.

01:04:07.666 --> 01:04:11.075
[APPLAUSE]

01:04:20.830 --> 01:04:25.120
CHOMSKY: Well, I won't
try to react to that.

01:04:25.120 --> 01:04:29.250
I do, however, have one
advantage over Hilary.

01:04:29.250 --> 01:04:30.720
I'm not going to
have any problems

01:04:30.720 --> 01:04:33.720
with my transparencies,
because I've

01:04:33.720 --> 01:04:38.421
managed to get through 50
years without having had any.

01:04:38.421 --> 01:04:41.460
And I have one
advantage over Steve,

01:04:41.460 --> 01:04:43.200
and namely I'm not
even going to try

01:04:43.200 --> 01:04:47.580
to answer the question that was
posed, which makes life easier.

01:04:51.540 --> 01:04:55.380
Conclusions about
human nature are

01:04:55.380 --> 01:04:59.430
likely to have large
scale human consequences,

01:04:59.430 --> 01:05:03.180
and therefore, they have to be
advanced with scrupulous care

01:05:03.180 --> 01:05:07.980
and with a recognition of
how little is understood

01:05:07.980 --> 01:05:10.950
when we pass beyond
fairly simple systems.

01:05:10.950 --> 01:05:15.240
That's true elsewhere in
the organic world as well.

01:05:15.240 --> 01:05:19.410
I don't feel that the question
that's raised for discussion

01:05:19.410 --> 01:05:22.560
can be answered
with much confidence

01:05:22.560 --> 01:05:26.490
in the areas that are
of greatest concern

01:05:26.490 --> 01:05:30.210
and significance for
humans, at least not by me.

01:05:30.210 --> 01:05:34.140
So I'm going to keep to a
few words about the questions

01:05:34.140 --> 01:05:38.650
rather than the answers, and
how they've been conceived,

01:05:38.650 --> 01:05:41.550
and the kinds of
problems they raise

01:05:41.550 --> 01:05:43.770
when we do try to address them.

01:05:43.770 --> 01:05:46.740
Well, at a very general
level, the question

01:05:46.740 --> 01:05:52.970
falls within a familiar pattern
of inquiry in general biology.

01:05:52.970 --> 01:05:55.380
We're interested in
determining what's sometimes

01:05:55.380 --> 01:05:58.140
called the norms
of reaction, that

01:05:58.140 --> 01:06:02.370
is the mapping of an environment
into a phenotype, which

01:06:02.370 --> 01:06:05.310
corresponds to a
genetic constitution,

01:06:05.310 --> 01:06:07.440
particular genetic constitution.

01:06:07.440 --> 01:06:10.770
I happen to be paraphrasing
a well-known evolutionary

01:06:10.770 --> 01:06:13.590
biologist, but the
point is familiar.

01:06:13.590 --> 01:06:16.020
And we can understand
human nature

01:06:16.020 --> 01:06:20.490
to be the norms of reaction of
the species abstracting away

01:06:20.490 --> 01:06:23.940
from variation, which
is probably fair enough

01:06:23.940 --> 01:06:26.880
in the general study of
humans since variability seems

01:06:26.880 --> 01:06:32.400
to be fairly small, certainly
relative to our understanding,

01:06:32.400 --> 01:06:37.710
but even relative to
closely related species.

01:06:37.710 --> 01:06:42.810
The particularly
informative in this quest

01:06:42.810 --> 01:06:48.210
is a phenomenon that's
been called canalization

01:06:48.210 --> 01:06:52.560
by biologist C. H.
Waddington some years ago.

01:06:52.560 --> 01:06:56.640
That's a process whereby
the end state of development

01:06:56.640 --> 01:07:01.030
is relatively independent of
environmental perturbations,

01:07:01.030 --> 01:07:02.790
so therefore,
heavily constrained

01:07:02.790 --> 01:07:06.780
by the genetic endowment
and particularly informative

01:07:06.780 --> 01:07:07.980
about it.

01:07:07.980 --> 01:07:10.320
These ideas are actually
reminiscent, closely

01:07:10.320 --> 01:07:12.960
reminiscent, I think, of
traditional rationalist

01:07:12.960 --> 01:07:17.730
conceptions of the origin and
nature of mental faculties.

01:07:17.730 --> 01:07:21.480
These efforts,
17th, 18th century,

01:07:21.480 --> 01:07:25.590
also concentrated on
what appeared to be fixed

01:07:25.590 --> 01:07:28.710
and highly structured
outcomes that

01:07:28.710 --> 01:07:31.965
were thought to vary little
with experience, probably

01:07:31.965 --> 01:07:36.390
incorrectly, called
common notions

01:07:36.390 --> 01:07:39.330
since the early 17th century.

01:07:39.330 --> 01:07:43.350
This whole perspective
became influential

01:07:43.350 --> 01:07:47.130
in general human
psychology in the 1950s,

01:07:47.130 --> 01:07:51.390
in part that was under
the influence of ethology,

01:07:51.390 --> 01:07:53.940
comparative zoology.

01:07:53.940 --> 01:07:58.620
At first, there was a good
deal of debate over the way

01:07:58.620 --> 01:08:01.950
the proposals were formulated.

01:08:01.950 --> 01:08:05.760
Konrad Lorenz, who was
particularly influential,

01:08:05.760 --> 01:08:10.890
insisted that specific
behaviors were innate,

01:08:10.890 --> 01:08:15.570
by which he meant that the
actual behavioral outcome is

01:08:15.570 --> 01:08:17.490
genetically determined.

01:08:17.490 --> 01:08:22.680
And as was quickly pointed
out, that no one expects--

01:08:22.680 --> 01:08:25.109
no biological trade
is expected to be

01:08:25.109 --> 01:08:29.340
a product of the
genetic endowment alone,

01:08:29.340 --> 01:08:33.600
or trivially a product
of the environment alone.

01:08:33.600 --> 01:08:39.960
The outcome is expected to be
some complicated interaction,

01:08:39.960 --> 01:08:42.347
interplay of genetic
constitution,

01:08:42.347 --> 01:08:44.430
and the environment, of
course, of the environment

01:08:44.430 --> 01:08:47.880
with developmental
interactions and processes

01:08:47.880 --> 01:08:51.630
that remain rather
poorly understood.

01:08:51.630 --> 01:08:54.000
To discover the
genetic constitution,

01:08:54.000 --> 01:08:57.689
these various factors have
to be disentangled somehow.

01:08:57.689 --> 01:09:02.430
And it's valuable to
apply the approach,

01:09:02.430 --> 01:09:05.760
the traditional rationalist
approach, the canalization

01:09:05.760 --> 01:09:08.310
approach, and to
take a close look

01:09:08.310 --> 01:09:11.609
at possible ranges of outcomes.

01:09:11.609 --> 01:09:16.000
The more highly structured
and restricted they are,

01:09:16.000 --> 01:09:24.029
the more richly
articulated and canalized,

01:09:24.029 --> 01:09:26.279
the more we're likely be
able to find something out

01:09:26.279 --> 01:09:27.939
about the organism.

01:09:27.939 --> 01:09:30.060
Well, the logic of
the situation dictates

01:09:30.060 --> 01:09:33.899
that the endowment
that determines

01:09:33.899 --> 01:09:37.290
what the organism can become,
the scope of possibilities

01:09:37.290 --> 01:09:40.960
also determines the
limitations of development.

01:09:40.960 --> 01:09:44.069
So, for example,
the same endowment

01:09:44.069 --> 01:09:46.830
that makes it possible
for a certain embryo

01:09:46.830 --> 01:09:49.319
to become a mouse is
going to prevent it

01:09:49.319 --> 01:09:52.090
from becoming a bird.

01:09:52.090 --> 01:09:53.830
And we should expect
the same to be true

01:09:53.830 --> 01:09:58.240
when we approach higher mental
faculties across the board,

01:09:58.240 --> 01:09:59.520
in fact.

01:09:59.520 --> 01:10:01.350
So for example, it
ought to be true

01:10:01.350 --> 01:10:05.070
when we consider the largely
unknown capacities that

01:10:05.070 --> 01:10:09.810
enable humans to acquire
deep understanding

01:10:09.810 --> 01:10:12.570
in certain domains of inquiry.

01:10:12.570 --> 01:10:15.750
And by the same
token, prevent this

01:10:15.750 --> 01:10:19.500
in some other, least
possible domains of inquiry,

01:10:19.500 --> 01:10:24.150
which might be actual, and
which might even be important,

01:10:24.150 --> 01:10:26.220
and which might,
for example, include

01:10:26.220 --> 01:10:28.470
the study of human nature.

01:10:28.470 --> 01:10:32.700
The human nature
raises questions

01:10:32.700 --> 01:10:36.810
that we may not have
intelligence enough to answer.

01:10:36.810 --> 01:10:41.070
Descartes speculated,
maybe rightly,

01:10:41.070 --> 01:10:43.860
and maybe it's not a matter
of enough intelligence.

01:10:43.860 --> 01:10:46.740
Now we look at it differently,
but the wrong kind

01:10:46.740 --> 01:10:48.210
of intelligence.

01:10:48.210 --> 01:10:51.690
Not an option for Descartes.

01:10:51.690 --> 01:10:56.020
Such an intrinsic
structure of intelligence,

01:10:56.020 --> 01:11:00.803
which has to be there, should
not occasion regret the--

01:11:05.510 --> 01:11:09.335
throughout the biological
world, the limitations

01:11:09.335 --> 01:11:12.260
that follow from the very
same endowment that makes

01:11:12.260 --> 01:11:15.210
possible rich--

01:11:15.210 --> 01:11:17.220
and states rich outcomes.

01:11:17.220 --> 01:11:20.360
So it should be an
occasion for celebration.

01:11:20.360 --> 01:11:22.760
But there are things we can't
do, even if some of them

01:11:22.760 --> 01:11:26.270
turn out to be things
we might like to do.

01:11:26.270 --> 01:11:28.940
Well, that's been the framework
for the study of language

01:11:28.940 --> 01:11:31.550
for about the last half century.

01:11:31.550 --> 01:11:34.070
The norms of
reaction, or sometimes

01:11:34.070 --> 01:11:37.070
called a language acquisition
device, what Steve called

01:11:37.070 --> 01:11:41.720
the language instinct, the
human genetic endowment, that

01:11:41.720 --> 01:11:44.660
determines a specific
language given

01:11:44.660 --> 01:11:49.970
particular course of experience,
and by the same token,

01:11:49.970 --> 01:11:52.760
prevents other
language like systems

01:11:52.760 --> 01:11:55.190
from developing in the mind.

01:11:55.190 --> 01:11:59.830
About these aspects of human
nature, a fair amount is known,

01:11:59.830 --> 01:12:02.290
at least, more or
less, understood,

01:12:02.290 --> 01:12:05.060
but I'm not going
to talk about that.

01:12:05.060 --> 01:12:08.330
In principle, it ought to
be possible to approach

01:12:08.330 --> 01:12:11.690
other higher mental
faculties in the same way.

01:12:11.690 --> 01:12:15.470
In practice, the difficulties
mount very quickly.

01:12:15.470 --> 01:12:18.590
Language appears to
be relatively isolated

01:12:18.590 --> 01:12:20.900
from other cognitive capacities.

01:12:20.900 --> 01:12:24.140
I'm referring here to its
structure, not its use,

01:12:24.140 --> 01:12:27.130
and not to components of
the structure, which may

01:12:27.130 --> 01:12:29.600
be shared all over the place.

01:12:29.600 --> 01:12:33.620
When you turn to other
aspects of mind, for example,

01:12:33.620 --> 01:12:36.650
or moral nature,
it's much harder

01:12:36.650 --> 01:12:41.900
to isolate components
for separate study,

01:12:41.900 --> 01:12:46.175
abstracting them from reflective
thought and many other factors.

01:12:46.175 --> 01:12:47.840
Now, nonetheless,
the topics have been

01:12:47.840 --> 01:12:50.700
investigated in various ways.

01:12:50.700 --> 01:12:53.330
There have been illuminating
thought experiments.

01:12:53.330 --> 01:12:56.870
There's been some experimental
work with children.

01:12:56.870 --> 01:12:59.810
There's comparative
studies of cross-cultural

01:12:59.810 --> 01:13:04.430
and even extending
to other species.

01:13:04.430 --> 01:13:10.700
Not uncommonly, the real world
offers occasions, instances,

01:13:10.700 --> 01:13:13.550
illustrations of how
the faculties function.

01:13:13.550 --> 01:13:15.570
Often, these are
painful choices.

01:13:15.570 --> 01:13:19.100
And probably, saw a
couple of weeks ago that

01:13:19.100 --> 01:13:23.690
in England British judges and
along with moral philosophers

01:13:23.690 --> 01:13:27.080
and the church and
many others were

01:13:27.080 --> 01:13:29.060
agonizing over the
question of whether it's

01:13:29.060 --> 01:13:35.420
legitimate to murder a child,
one of two Siamese twins,

01:13:35.420 --> 01:13:39.890
in order that the other might
have a chance to survive.

01:13:39.890 --> 01:13:42.960
Questions like these
test our moral faculties,

01:13:42.960 --> 01:13:46.610
a hard test in this case,
maybe impossible one.

01:13:46.610 --> 01:13:50.240
And pursuing them may enable
us to discover something

01:13:50.240 --> 01:13:51.260
about their nature.

01:13:51.260 --> 01:13:54.770
That's the kind of
thought experiment

01:13:54.770 --> 01:13:57.740
that has been quite
interestingly pursued.

01:14:00.490 --> 01:14:02.950
Sometimes this
perspective, this generally

01:14:02.950 --> 01:14:04.960
ecological perspective,
if you like,

01:14:04.960 --> 01:14:06.430
is counter-opposed
to another one,

01:14:06.430 --> 01:14:10.720
it's called a relativistic
one, which in an extreme form

01:14:10.720 --> 01:14:15.730
holds that, apart from the basic
physical structure of a person,

01:14:15.730 --> 01:14:17.260
humans have no nature.

01:14:17.260 --> 01:14:19.900
They only have history.

01:14:19.900 --> 01:14:25.660
Their thought and their
behavior can be modified

01:14:25.660 --> 01:14:27.940
at will and without limit.

01:14:27.940 --> 01:14:30.860
Actually, nothing like
that can be literally true,

01:14:30.860 --> 01:14:35.670
but such views are rather
widely articulated in some form.

01:14:35.670 --> 01:14:38.900
In a version that's due
to Richard Rorty, he

01:14:38.900 --> 01:14:41.320
whom I'm quoting
now, "humans have

01:14:41.320 --> 01:14:43.930
extraordinary malleability.

01:14:43.930 --> 01:14:45.610
We are coming to
think of ourselves

01:14:45.610 --> 01:14:50.020
as the flexible protean
self-shaping animal,

01:14:50.020 --> 01:14:52.810
rather than as having
specific instincts.

01:14:52.810 --> 01:14:56.290
Therefore, there can be no
moral progress in human affairs.

01:14:56.290 --> 01:14:59.180
Just different ways
of looking at things.

01:14:59.180 --> 01:15:01.690
We should put aside
the vain effort

01:15:01.690 --> 01:15:04.540
at exploration of
our moral nature,

01:15:04.540 --> 01:15:06.550
or reasoned argument about it.

01:15:06.550 --> 01:15:09.940
We should keep, to what he
calls, manipulating sentiment,

01:15:09.940 --> 01:15:14.440
if we happen to be for or
against torture, or massacre,

01:15:14.440 --> 01:15:16.090
or whatever."

01:15:16.090 --> 01:15:18.400
I'm probably misinterpreting.

01:15:18.400 --> 01:15:22.820
I can't imagine that the words
mean what they seem to say,

01:15:22.820 --> 01:15:27.210
but at least that's what
they seem to me to say.

01:15:27.210 --> 01:15:31.800
These proposals have,
maybe not surprisingly,

01:15:31.800 --> 01:15:33.930
evoked a good deal of criticism.

01:15:33.930 --> 01:15:36.840
One recent paper in the
philosophical foundations

01:15:36.840 --> 01:15:40.170
of human rights, discussing
Rorty and others,

01:15:40.170 --> 01:15:42.930
suggest, and quoting
again, "that nobody

01:15:42.930 --> 01:15:45.750
would have taken a
Nazi seriously who

01:15:45.750 --> 01:15:49.110
had claimed in 1945
that the sole basis

01:15:49.110 --> 01:15:51.780
for moral condemnation
of the Holocaust

01:15:51.780 --> 01:15:55.620
is just culturally relative
emotional manipulation based

01:15:55.620 --> 01:15:58.830
on shrewdly devised
sentimental stories."

01:15:58.830 --> 01:16:05.460
Well, if that's so, and I assume
it is so, we want to know why.

01:16:05.460 --> 01:16:06.900
Why is that so?

01:16:06.900 --> 01:16:11.100
And if nobody, the
word nobody includes

01:16:11.100 --> 01:16:14.310
all normal human
beings, well, we're

01:16:14.310 --> 01:16:16.350
back to the question,
the hard question

01:16:16.350 --> 01:16:20.130
of intrinsic human nature,
back to the question, but not

01:16:20.130 --> 01:16:22.110
an answer.

01:16:22.110 --> 01:16:26.980
This notion of unique
human malleability,

01:16:26.980 --> 01:16:30.610
contrary to what Rorty
suggests, is not at all novel.

01:16:30.610 --> 01:16:32.460
It's quite conventional.

01:16:32.460 --> 01:16:36.210
It goes at least
back to the man,

01:16:36.210 --> 01:16:38.550
beasts, machine
controversies that

01:16:38.550 --> 01:16:43.080
were inspired by Descartes.

01:16:43.080 --> 01:16:48.060
Here's one typical
example, argument, in 1740

01:16:48.060 --> 01:16:52.560
by James Harris,
English philosopher,

01:16:52.560 --> 01:16:55.800
saying that, unlike
animals and machines,

01:16:55.800 --> 01:16:58.890
the leading principle
of man is multi-form,

01:16:58.890 --> 01:17:03.090
originally uninstructed,
pliant, and docile.

01:17:03.090 --> 01:17:07.080
Weakness of instinct
leads to vast variety,

01:17:07.080 --> 01:17:10.200
but also to extreme
malleability.

01:17:10.200 --> 01:17:13.650
That's an idea that has a
long and quite inglorious

01:17:13.650 --> 01:17:15.970
history since that time.

01:17:15.970 --> 01:17:18.800
Well, with no metric,
no way of measuring

01:17:18.800 --> 01:17:23.220
what how variable
humans are as compared

01:17:23.220 --> 01:17:25.830
with others and with very
little understanding,

01:17:25.830 --> 01:17:29.430
it's pretty hard to make
any sense of such judgments.

01:17:29.430 --> 01:17:34.710
But whatever merit
they have, they

01:17:34.710 --> 01:17:39.240
don't seem to seriously offer
an alternative to the approach

01:17:39.240 --> 01:17:43.680
that I outlined before this
roughly ethological approach.

01:17:43.680 --> 01:17:46.650
We can take for granted,
it's undoubtedly true,

01:17:46.650 --> 01:17:49.890
that a person's understanding
and judgment, and values,

01:17:49.890 --> 01:17:54.150
and goals reflect the
acquired culture, norms,

01:17:54.150 --> 01:17:55.650
and conventions.

01:17:55.650 --> 01:17:57.720
But how are these acquired?

01:17:57.720 --> 01:18:00.910
They are not acquired
by taking a pill.

01:18:00.910 --> 01:18:04.260
They are constructed by the
mind somehow on the basis

01:18:04.260 --> 01:18:08.820
of scattered experience,
which they are constantly

01:18:08.820 --> 01:18:14.050
applied in circumstances that
are novel and complicated.

01:18:14.050 --> 01:18:15.480
These are important facts.

01:18:15.480 --> 01:18:17.880
They were discussed
about the same time

01:18:17.880 --> 01:18:22.680
as James Harris more than 250
years ago and by David Hume.

01:18:22.680 --> 01:18:26.490
He pointed out that the number
of our duties is in a manner

01:18:26.490 --> 01:18:31.170
infinite, and therefore, just
as in other parts of the study

01:18:31.170 --> 01:18:35.120
of nature, we should try
to-- we should seek--

01:18:35.120 --> 01:18:37.920
I'm quoting now-- "general
principles upon which

01:18:37.920 --> 01:18:41.040
all of our notions of
morals are founded,

01:18:41.040 --> 01:18:45.240
principles that are original
instincts of the human mind,

01:18:45.240 --> 01:18:49.590
enhanced by reflection,
but steadfast and immutable

01:18:49.590 --> 01:18:52.630
as components of
fixed human nature."

01:18:52.630 --> 01:18:55.200
If we don't do that, it's hard
to see how we can make sense

01:18:55.200 --> 01:19:00.450
out of the fact that our
duties are commonly new,

01:19:00.450 --> 01:19:03.840
we understand them,
we respond to them

01:19:03.840 --> 01:19:09.570
in complicated and
novel circumstances.

01:19:09.570 --> 01:19:13.530
Well, like Adam Smith,
Hume took sympathy to be,

01:19:13.530 --> 01:19:16.860
as he called it, a very powerful
principle in human nature

01:19:16.860 --> 01:19:19.140
and the grounding of much else.

01:19:19.140 --> 01:19:23.260
That idea was reconstructed
in a Darwinian framework

01:19:23.260 --> 01:19:27.810
about 100 years ago by the
anarchist, natural historian,

01:19:27.810 --> 01:19:30.750
and zoologist Peter
Kropotkin, what

01:19:30.750 --> 01:19:34.860
you might call the founding
volume of the sociobiology,

01:19:34.860 --> 01:19:37.450
or evolutionary psychology.

01:19:37.450 --> 01:19:39.390
There is recent
work which suggests

01:19:39.390 --> 01:19:42.570
some possible evolutionary
scenarios that might

01:19:42.570 --> 01:19:44.610
have led to this development.

01:19:44.610 --> 01:19:47.250
There's a review of
current work on the topic

01:19:47.250 --> 01:19:50.970
in a recent book edited by
Leonard Katz of the philosophy

01:19:50.970 --> 01:19:52.980
department here.

01:19:52.980 --> 01:19:57.390
To this picture, we should
add some other conceptions

01:19:57.390 --> 01:20:00.420
that have been very fruitfully
studied experimentally

01:20:00.420 --> 01:20:02.290
in the past several decades.

01:20:02.290 --> 01:20:07.170
And I think can reasonably also
be traced back to 17th century

01:20:07.170 --> 01:20:10.320
origins of modern science,
including the brain

01:20:10.320 --> 01:20:12.270
and cognitive sciences.

01:20:12.270 --> 01:20:14.850
One strand of this
is the recognition

01:20:14.850 --> 01:20:18.450
that innate capacities
are only latent.

01:20:18.450 --> 01:20:22.290
They have to be triggered by
experience to be manifested.

01:20:22.290 --> 01:20:27.080
To borrow Descartes analogy,
innate ideas, he said,

01:20:27.080 --> 01:20:30.260
are like a susceptibility
to a disease.

01:20:30.260 --> 01:20:33.270
The susceptibility's innate,
but you don't get the disease

01:20:33.270 --> 01:20:34.920
unless something happens.

01:20:34.920 --> 01:20:38.680
That's what he meant
by innate ideas.

01:20:38.680 --> 01:20:45.840
Another fruitful idea that
was well examined extensively

01:20:45.840 --> 01:20:49.530
in the 17th century is that
the phenomena of the world

01:20:49.530 --> 01:20:53.250
don't actually
constitute experience,

01:20:53.250 --> 01:20:58.740
rather they become experience,
as our modes of cognition

01:20:58.740 --> 01:21:01.860
construct them in specific
ways, and therefore, they

01:21:01.860 --> 01:21:06.310
must conform to those
modes of cognition.

01:21:06.310 --> 01:21:09.450
These modes of cognition
are a distinctive property

01:21:09.450 --> 01:21:10.200
of our nature.

01:21:10.200 --> 01:21:12.680
They differ for
different organisms.

01:21:12.680 --> 01:21:15.870
They're what Lawrence,
back in the 1930s,

01:21:15.870 --> 01:21:19.230
called a kind of biological
a priori differing

01:21:19.230 --> 01:21:21.250
for different organisms.

01:21:21.250 --> 01:21:24.030
And that's also true of
the experience that's

01:21:24.030 --> 01:21:27.420
the basis for the rich
mental constructions

01:21:27.420 --> 01:21:32.340
that we call cultures,
or norms, or conventions.

01:21:32.340 --> 01:21:35.490
The process of
mental construction

01:21:35.490 --> 01:21:37.830
of experience and
interpretation of it

01:21:37.830 --> 01:21:43.990
based on a fairly common
genetic constitution,

01:21:43.990 --> 01:21:48.090
well, it must be
rich to the extent

01:21:48.090 --> 01:21:51.960
that the structures, the
outcomes are highly structured

01:21:51.960 --> 01:21:55.740
and constrained and relatively
invariant, canalized,

01:21:55.740 --> 01:21:56.700
if you like.

01:21:56.700 --> 01:21:58.530
And that appears
to be invariably

01:21:58.530 --> 01:22:01.710
the case when, at least,
anything we understand.

01:22:01.710 --> 01:22:04.770
And if that's so, then
the relativist approach

01:22:04.770 --> 01:22:07.100
is going to turn out to
be profoundly innatist,

01:22:07.100 --> 01:22:10.920
at least if it is to address
the kinds of questions that

01:22:10.920 --> 01:22:13.950
are studied elsewhere,
the issues of acquisition

01:22:13.950 --> 01:22:16.740
and nature and use
of attained systems,

01:22:16.740 --> 01:22:19.410
as, say, in the study
of the visual system,

01:22:19.410 --> 01:22:22.980
or linguistic systems, or
other products of organisms.

01:22:22.980 --> 01:22:24.630
Actually, I doubt
that there really

01:22:24.630 --> 01:22:29.520
are conflicts of principle
separating these apparently

01:22:29.520 --> 01:22:33.030
contrasting views,
rather emphasis on one

01:22:33.030 --> 01:22:36.030
or another aspect of what
an individual person is,

01:22:36.030 --> 01:22:39.210
and, of course, empirical
questions, a vast number

01:22:39.210 --> 01:22:44.160
and few answers about irrelevant
aspects of human nature.

01:22:44.160 --> 01:22:46.850
Well, last word, final
word on the import

01:22:46.850 --> 01:22:50.310
of any tentative conclusions
one might entertain

01:22:50.310 --> 01:22:53.790
about human nature, one way
to assess their importance

01:22:53.790 --> 01:22:58.260
is to observe how deeply they
enter into conceptions of right

01:22:58.260 --> 01:23:02.220
and justice and actual
struggles that these conceptions

01:23:02.220 --> 01:23:03.450
engendered.

01:23:03.450 --> 01:23:06.120
So examples of the kind I
mentioned in the British case,

01:23:06.120 --> 01:23:10.440
well, may be unusual, such
cases do arise quite commonly

01:23:10.440 --> 01:23:11.250
in our lives.

01:23:11.250 --> 01:23:14.400
That's essentially what
Hume was talking about.

01:23:14.400 --> 01:23:18.420
More generally, every approach
to how human relations should

01:23:18.420 --> 01:23:22.290
be arranged, whether it's
revolutionary, or reformist,

01:23:22.290 --> 01:23:26.580
or committed to
stability, it is based

01:23:26.580 --> 01:23:30.810
on some conception of human
nature, maybe tacit conception.

01:23:30.810 --> 01:23:33.390
If it has any claim
to moral standing,

01:23:33.390 --> 01:23:36.870
it's put forth with
at least tacit claim

01:23:36.870 --> 01:23:39.300
that it's beneficial
to humans, meaning

01:23:39.300 --> 01:23:41.050
because of their nature.

01:23:41.050 --> 01:23:45.060
So for example, take Adam
Smith's harsh condemnation

01:23:45.060 --> 01:23:47.910
of division of labor
and his insistence

01:23:47.910 --> 01:23:52.140
that in any civilized society,
government must, in his words,

01:23:52.140 --> 01:23:58.650
take pains to restrict it and
mitigate its harmful effects.

01:23:58.650 --> 01:24:02.790
That conviction was based on an
empirical assumption, his words

01:24:02.790 --> 01:24:06.060
that the man whose life
is spent performing

01:24:06.060 --> 01:24:08.490
a few simple
operations will become

01:24:08.490 --> 01:24:11.550
as stupid and ignorant as it's
possible for a human creature

01:24:11.550 --> 01:24:15.900
to be, which is morally
impermissible, he argued,

01:24:15.900 --> 01:24:18.900
because it violates fundamental
human rights grounded

01:24:18.900 --> 01:24:20.820
in human nature.

01:24:20.820 --> 01:24:24.360
Like other capacities,
human understanding

01:24:24.360 --> 01:24:27.690
will not flourish
along its own course

01:24:27.690 --> 01:24:30.070
without external triggering.

01:24:30.070 --> 01:24:33.810
It's like in Smith's
words, without the occasion

01:24:33.810 --> 01:24:36.840
to exercise the understanding,
and therefore, people

01:24:36.840 --> 01:24:39.270
must have the occasion,
or it's an infringement

01:24:39.270 --> 01:24:42.120
on their fundamental right.

01:24:42.120 --> 01:24:45.480
That's a pretty standard
enlightenment doctrine.

01:24:45.480 --> 01:24:48.930
Another one of the founders of
classical liberalism, Wilhelm

01:24:48.930 --> 01:24:54.180
von Humboldt, argued that humans
are born to inquire and create

01:24:54.180 --> 01:25:01.410
an infringement on the
natural, this natural right,

01:25:01.410 --> 01:25:03.480
is unacceptable.

01:25:03.480 --> 01:25:06.120
An infringement on that
right would prevent them

01:25:06.120 --> 01:25:09.870
from exercising those intrinsic
capacities to the fullest

01:25:09.870 --> 01:25:11.470
extent possible.

01:25:11.470 --> 01:25:14.190
Hence, illegitimate.

01:25:14.190 --> 01:25:18.090
Accordingly, as he put it, if an
artisan produces beautiful work

01:25:18.090 --> 01:25:21.480
on command, we may admire
what he does, but we

01:25:21.480 --> 01:25:25.560
will despise what he is, a
tool in the hands of others

01:25:25.560 --> 01:25:29.160
and not a free human being
acting in voluntary association

01:25:29.160 --> 01:25:31.260
with other free people.

01:25:31.260 --> 01:25:35.790
The strong popular resistance
to the modern industrial system

01:25:35.790 --> 01:25:39.750
was inspired by
similar conceptions.

01:25:39.750 --> 01:25:42.300
When the system was taking
shape right around here

01:25:42.300 --> 01:25:45.710
in eastern Massachusetts
150 years ago,

01:25:45.710 --> 01:25:48.520
there was a lively
independent press

01:25:48.520 --> 01:25:51.880
run by artisans and shop
workers, many of them

01:25:51.880 --> 01:25:54.490
young women from nearby farms.

01:25:54.490 --> 01:25:57.200
And it makes interesting
reading today.

01:25:57.200 --> 01:26:01.280
They weren't borrowing from
any intellectual tradition.

01:26:01.280 --> 01:26:04.590
They were expressing themselves.

01:26:04.590 --> 01:26:05.590
These are mostly quotes.

01:26:05.590 --> 01:26:07.780
The contributors
denounced what they

01:26:07.780 --> 01:26:11.530
called the new spirit of the
age, gain wealth forgetting

01:26:11.530 --> 01:26:13.270
all but self.

01:26:13.270 --> 01:26:16.300
They condemn the loss of
dignity and independence

01:26:16.300 --> 01:26:19.930
of Rights and Freedoms,
the degradation and loss

01:26:19.930 --> 01:26:24.550
of self-respect, the decline of
culture, skill, and attainment,

01:26:24.550 --> 01:26:26.530
as they were subjected
to what they called

01:26:26.530 --> 01:26:30.730
wage slavery, forced
to sell themselves,

01:26:30.730 --> 01:26:35.320
not what they produced, becoming
menials and humble subjects

01:26:35.320 --> 01:26:39.010
of despots, reduced to
a state of servitude

01:26:39.010 --> 01:26:42.010
that was not very different
from chattel slavery

01:26:42.010 --> 01:26:43.520
of the southern plantations.

01:26:43.520 --> 01:26:47.670
So they felt-- these
are quotes, actually.

01:26:47.670 --> 01:26:50.730
And those ideas were not at
all uncommon at the time.

01:26:50.730 --> 01:26:53.070
They were upheld in large
part by the Republican Party,

01:26:53.070 --> 01:26:54.710
for example.

01:26:54.710 --> 01:26:57.900
And they were of profound
concern to the masters,

01:26:57.900 --> 01:27:00.630
huge capital and
intellectual resources

01:27:00.630 --> 01:27:02.910
have gone into the
effort to drive them out

01:27:02.910 --> 01:27:06.000
of people's minds,
very consciously.

01:27:06.000 --> 01:27:09.390
This goes back to the early days
of the Industrial Revolution.

01:27:09.390 --> 01:27:12.120
And dramatically, so
in the past century,

01:27:12.120 --> 01:27:14.220
when one of the
major industries,

01:27:14.220 --> 01:27:16.890
modern industries, public
relations industry,

01:27:16.890 --> 01:27:20.190
was created, essentially
for that purpose.

01:27:20.190 --> 01:27:23.370
In the words of some
of its leading figures

01:27:23.370 --> 01:27:25.680
back in the 1920s,
it was created

01:27:25.680 --> 01:27:28.320
to regiment the
public mind every bit

01:27:28.320 --> 01:27:31.890
as much as an army regiments
the body of its soldiers

01:27:31.890 --> 01:27:34.770
to impose a
philosophy of futility

01:27:34.770 --> 01:27:37.560
and a focus on the
superficial things of life,

01:27:37.560 --> 01:27:40.740
like fashionable consumption,
and to prevent people

01:27:40.740 --> 01:27:43.260
from harming themselves
by taking charge

01:27:43.260 --> 01:27:46.590
of their own affairs, just
as a responsible parent

01:27:46.590 --> 01:27:51.270
would prevent a child from
running into a busy street.

01:27:51.270 --> 01:27:55.650
All of this was justified
explicitly as good for people,

01:27:55.650 --> 01:27:58.770
reflecting human
nature, and reflecting

01:27:58.770 --> 01:28:02.880
the fundamental inadequacies
of the less intelligent,

01:28:02.880 --> 01:28:05.880
including their
unique malleability.

01:28:05.880 --> 01:28:07.860
And that's a crucial
assumption that

01:28:07.860 --> 01:28:11.340
removes any moral barriers
rooted in human nature that

01:28:11.340 --> 01:28:13.860
might be conceived
in different ways as,

01:28:13.860 --> 01:28:16.230
indeed, it was conceived
by the subjects

01:28:16.230 --> 01:28:20.220
of these benign manipulations.

01:28:20.220 --> 01:28:23.460
Conflicts over human nature,
what are in our tradition

01:28:23.460 --> 01:28:26.130
called inalienable
rights, they lie

01:28:26.130 --> 01:28:29.520
at the heart of the slow
evolution of the human rights

01:28:29.520 --> 01:28:32.970
culture over the
past few centuries.

01:28:32.970 --> 01:28:37.290
An optimist could hold,
maybe realistically,

01:28:37.290 --> 01:28:42.780
that history reveals a deepening
appreciation for human rights

01:28:42.780 --> 01:28:46.510
as well as considerable
broadening of their range,

01:28:46.510 --> 01:28:48.640
including in very recent years.

01:28:48.640 --> 01:28:52.320
This is not without reversals,
sometimes sharp reversals,

01:28:52.320 --> 01:28:57.300
but I think the general
tendency is detectable and real.

01:28:57.300 --> 01:29:01.090
Inquiry into these
slow changes--

01:29:01.090 --> 01:29:03.810
so, for example, the
debates over slavery,

01:29:03.810 --> 01:29:07.680
for example, which
were rational debates.

01:29:07.680 --> 01:29:09.750
Inquiry into these
slow changes suggest

01:29:09.750 --> 01:29:13.800
that what may have happened
could have been attainment

01:29:13.800 --> 01:29:17.790
of a clearer insight into
our own basic values rooted

01:29:17.790 --> 01:29:19.140
in our nature.

01:29:19.140 --> 01:29:22.320
These are issues that are
very much alive today.

01:29:22.320 --> 01:29:26.700
For example, conflicts over
ratification of human rights

01:29:26.700 --> 01:29:30.670
conventions, or to take
a current case very sharp

01:29:30.670 --> 01:29:33.000
and divide in the
world today over

01:29:33.000 --> 01:29:36.210
unilateral military
intervention for allegedly

01:29:36.210 --> 01:29:38.610
humanitarian reasons.

01:29:38.610 --> 01:29:43.140
I think a case can be made that
these are the appropriate terms

01:29:43.140 --> 01:29:47.070
for framing the issues, to
some extent, comprehending

01:29:47.070 --> 01:29:48.240
the history.

01:29:48.240 --> 01:29:52.200
And it's at least imaginable,
although hardly imminent,

01:29:52.200 --> 01:29:56.190
that scientific inquiry
might offer some guidelines

01:29:56.190 --> 01:29:59.640
for approaching the extremely
significant issues that

01:29:59.640 --> 01:30:04.350
arise, perhaps, offering
some answers to questions

01:30:04.350 --> 01:30:08.580
that, as far as I can see, we
can only speculate about today.

01:30:08.580 --> 01:30:09.570
Thanks.

01:30:09.570 --> 01:30:13.035
[APPLAUSE]

01:30:23.960 --> 01:30:24.710
KEYSER: All right.

01:30:24.710 --> 01:30:27.510
So now we're open for questions.

01:30:27.510 --> 01:30:33.920
And if you have a question,
please, maybe the best

01:30:33.920 --> 01:30:36.830
thing to do would be to
stand up and speak loudly,

01:30:36.830 --> 01:30:39.380
so that the entire
audience can hear.

01:30:39.380 --> 01:30:41.060
And you can address
it to the panel

01:30:41.060 --> 01:30:44.170
or to individuals on the panel.

01:30:44.170 --> 01:30:47.930
So we have about 20 minutes.

01:30:50.540 --> 01:30:52.430
Claude.

01:30:52.430 --> 01:30:54.260
AUDIENCE: I have a
question of Dr. Pinker,

01:30:54.260 --> 01:30:57.690
but also the panel at large.

01:30:57.690 --> 01:31:02.434
Your principles derived from
the Judeo-Christian theories

01:31:02.434 --> 01:31:04.904
around me.

01:31:04.904 --> 01:31:09.844
The entire model seems to
be relative to-- derived

01:31:09.844 --> 01:31:10.832
from western culture.

01:31:10.832 --> 01:31:12.950
On the parallels
in eastern culture,

01:31:12.950 --> 01:31:15.590
can we substitute
Buddhism, for example,

01:31:15.590 --> 01:31:18.500
Zoroastrianism,
Shintoism, and come up

01:31:18.500 --> 01:31:22.468
with a similar set
of conclusions?

01:31:22.468 --> 01:31:24.770
Oh, I'm done.

01:31:29.893 --> 01:31:32.060
PINKER: The question is,
are there parallel theories

01:31:32.060 --> 01:31:35.870
of human nature implicit in
the other major religious

01:31:35.870 --> 01:31:36.740
traditions?

01:31:36.740 --> 01:31:37.710
There certainly are.

01:31:37.710 --> 01:31:39.460
I'm very far from
an expert on them.

01:31:39.460 --> 01:31:41.990
But there is a wonderful book
called 10 Theories of Human

01:31:41.990 --> 01:31:46.850
Nature by Leslie Stevenson, and
I forget the co-authors name,

01:31:46.850 --> 01:31:51.230
of which, I think, 6
of them might belong

01:31:51.230 --> 01:31:54.050
to different religious
traditions, Judeo-Christian,

01:31:54.050 --> 01:31:56.660
Buddhist, Confucianist,
and so on, and then

01:31:56.660 --> 01:32:01.370
Marxism, psychoanalytic
approach, ethological approach,

01:32:01.370 --> 01:32:02.150
and so on.

01:32:02.150 --> 01:32:04.790
But certainly, I
think every, indeed,

01:32:04.790 --> 01:32:07.550
every religion does have a
theory of human nature embedded

01:32:07.550 --> 01:32:08.234
in it.

01:32:08.234 --> 01:32:09.890
AUDIENCE: Are they similar?

01:32:09.890 --> 01:32:13.973
PINKER: Oh, there are some--

01:32:13.973 --> 01:32:15.890
the best I can say is
that there are certainly

01:32:15.890 --> 01:32:17.030
some similarities.

01:32:17.030 --> 01:32:21.020
And the major proselytizing
religions, I think,

01:32:21.020 --> 01:32:25.250
all have some notion of
choice and a moral sense

01:32:25.250 --> 01:32:27.200
as part of human nature.

01:32:27.200 --> 01:32:30.800
They're not deterministic
in the literal sense

01:32:30.800 --> 01:32:34.850
because that would obviate the
need for a religion to tell you

01:32:34.850 --> 01:32:37.890
what to do, tell you how
to exercise that choice.

01:32:37.890 --> 01:32:39.560
But that's the main one.

01:32:39.560 --> 01:32:42.050
But, again, I'm not enough
of an expert to really answer

01:32:42.050 --> 01:32:43.930
that question intelligently.

01:32:43.930 --> 01:32:45.485
CHOMSKY: If I can add a--

01:32:45.485 --> 01:32:50.570
a good way to test the question
you're raising, one way to test

01:32:50.570 --> 01:32:52.790
it is to have a look
at the Universal

01:32:52.790 --> 01:32:55.070
Declaration of Human Rights.

01:32:55.070 --> 01:32:58.730
Human rights are founded in
some conception of human nature.

01:32:58.730 --> 01:33:01.430
And the history of the
Universal Declaration,

01:33:01.430 --> 01:33:05.180
the 1948 Declaration
reveals that there

01:33:05.180 --> 01:33:10.540
was very active contribution
from a wide variety of--

01:33:10.540 --> 01:33:14.630
in fact, any very broad variety
of religious and cultural

01:33:14.630 --> 01:33:15.290
traditions.

01:33:15.290 --> 01:33:18.000
It was not a reflection
of Western values,

01:33:18.000 --> 01:33:19.350
as sometimes said.

01:33:19.350 --> 01:33:23.870
And they managed to reach
a considerable consensus

01:33:23.870 --> 01:33:28.310
on a fairly detailed
accounting of human rights.

01:33:28.310 --> 01:33:30.900
Not that anyone lives
up to the principles

01:33:30.900 --> 01:33:35.060
but at least formulating
them as desiderata.

01:33:35.060 --> 01:33:36.830
PINKER: One other thing.

01:33:36.830 --> 01:33:41.390
Certainly, whenever there is an
articulation of what morality

01:33:41.390 --> 01:33:44.240
consists of, you
get some version

01:33:44.240 --> 01:33:47.180
of a kind of golden
rule ethics that

01:33:47.180 --> 01:33:49.820
is the interchangeability
of the interests and rights

01:33:49.820 --> 01:33:50.900
of different individuals.

01:33:50.900 --> 01:33:54.890
I can't expect you to
submit to something that I

01:33:54.890 --> 01:33:56.240
would refuse to submit to.

01:33:56.240 --> 01:33:58.340
And that, indeed,
seems to have been

01:33:58.340 --> 01:34:01.340
rediscovered in a great
many moral traditions.

01:34:01.340 --> 01:34:05.960
The philosopher Peter Singer in
his book The Expanding Circle

01:34:05.960 --> 01:34:08.150
argues that, in
fact, moral systems,

01:34:08.150 --> 01:34:11.450
the world over, tend to be quite
alike, except for one parameter

01:34:11.450 --> 01:34:14.150
of variation, almost like
the linguistic parameters

01:34:14.150 --> 01:34:15.440
that Noam introduced.

01:34:15.440 --> 01:34:18.800
And that is, what is the size
of the circle of entities

01:34:18.800 --> 01:34:22.430
that you count as persons and
whose interests you regard as

01:34:22.430 --> 01:34:24.540
interchangeable with one's own?

01:34:24.540 --> 01:34:28.910
And, in fact, a very simple,
but not oversimplified

01:34:28.910 --> 01:34:31.610
theory of the kind of moral
progress that Noam alluded to

01:34:31.610 --> 01:34:34.370
is that the circle has
gotten bigger and bigger,

01:34:34.370 --> 01:34:37.430
that it used to include
basically your own village

01:34:37.430 --> 01:34:40.160
and then it expanded
to include the tribe

01:34:40.160 --> 01:34:44.210
and both sexes and all races
and eventually all of humankind.

01:34:44.210 --> 01:34:47.330
And many current
moral debates consist

01:34:47.330 --> 01:34:49.310
of whether we should
expand the circle further

01:34:49.310 --> 01:34:53.330
to include, say, warm blooded
animals, or animals in general,

01:34:53.330 --> 01:34:56.060
or species, should
species be treated

01:34:56.060 --> 01:35:00.140
like individuals with a
right to exist and respect

01:35:00.140 --> 01:35:01.350
for their interests?

01:35:01.350 --> 01:35:05.060
But the psychology of how
we evaluate the moral claim

01:35:05.060 --> 01:35:08.330
seems to be remarkably
parallel modulo

01:35:08.330 --> 01:35:10.310
this parameter of how
big the circle is.

01:35:12.830 --> 01:35:14.990
PUTNAM: I guess, as a
professional philosopher,

01:35:14.990 --> 01:35:18.140
I want to be annoying
to Steve, and say, I

01:35:18.140 --> 01:35:20.600
think the talk of a
Judeo-Christian theory

01:35:20.600 --> 01:35:24.110
of human nature, I mean,
nothing comes to mind.

01:35:24.110 --> 01:35:26.390
For example, he meant,
well, there is a theory

01:35:26.390 --> 01:35:28.040
that humans have a moral sense.

01:35:28.040 --> 01:35:31.190
I'm trying to think whether the
whole Talmud, which certainly

01:35:31.190 --> 01:35:35.000
was the lead Jewish,
body of Jewish thought

01:35:35.000 --> 01:35:39.200
until the beginning
of the 19th century,

01:35:39.200 --> 01:35:43.893
contains a single expression I
would translate as moral sense.

01:35:43.893 --> 01:35:44.810
I don't think it does.

01:35:47.430 --> 01:35:49.980
There is nothing in Aristotle--

01:35:49.980 --> 01:35:52.940
moral sense theory is
an 18th century theory,

01:35:52.940 --> 01:35:55.145
a 17th, 18th century theory.

01:35:55.145 --> 01:35:57.590
It plays no role in Aquinas.

01:35:57.590 --> 01:36:02.840
If you move to the Greeks,
the big change, I think--

01:36:02.840 --> 01:36:05.900
you can't translate moral
sense into Aristotle's Greek,

01:36:05.900 --> 01:36:08.610
because moral didn't
have the same mean--

01:36:11.170 --> 01:36:14.600
moral is from Latin,
and it's from mores,

01:36:14.600 --> 01:36:16.550
and that's a translation
of the Greek ethics.

01:36:16.550 --> 01:36:19.220
But ethics did not--
but the ethical sense

01:36:19.220 --> 01:36:22.150
is not a term that
would make the slightest

01:36:22.150 --> 01:36:24.420
sense to Aristotle.

01:36:24.420 --> 01:36:28.690
Where I think there is an
interesting shift in the west--

01:36:32.040 --> 01:36:34.140
well, this is to
the east as well--

01:36:34.140 --> 01:36:37.950
for Aristotle, this
ethics had to do both

01:36:37.950 --> 01:36:39.710
with how we should
behave to others.

01:36:39.710 --> 01:36:41.130
And I agree with Steve--

01:36:41.130 --> 01:36:43.350
with the last thing
he said, is right.

01:36:43.350 --> 01:36:47.700
The universality of something,
like a golden rule principle,

01:36:47.700 --> 01:36:49.600
does seem to be very strong.

01:36:49.600 --> 01:36:54.810
But for Aristotle, the
tremendously important question

01:36:54.810 --> 01:36:58.770
was, what human
flourishing consists in,

01:36:58.770 --> 01:37:01.980
a question which is of great
importance to you and Chomsky.

01:37:01.980 --> 01:37:04.230
Eudaimonia is sometimes
translates as happiness.

01:37:04.230 --> 01:37:08.190
And Aristotle is really
human flourishing.

01:37:08.190 --> 01:37:10.200
And one effect of the
religions coming--

01:37:10.200 --> 01:37:13.050
I think one bad effect-- and I
say this as a practicing Jew--

01:37:13.050 --> 01:37:17.430
but one bad effect of
the rise of the victory

01:37:17.430 --> 01:37:20.070
of Christianity and
Judaism and so on

01:37:20.070 --> 01:37:21.938
is to suppress that
question somewhat,

01:37:21.938 --> 01:37:23.730
because it was supposed
to be obvious, what

01:37:23.730 --> 01:37:25.440
human flourishing consists in.

01:37:25.440 --> 01:37:28.380
And so make ethics almost
entirely synonymous

01:37:28.380 --> 01:37:31.097
with the question of what
are your duties to others

01:37:31.097 --> 01:37:33.180
to the point where it's
often said the notion of--

01:37:33.180 --> 01:37:37.380
Bentham said the notion of a
duty to yourself is incoherent.

01:37:37.380 --> 01:37:41.770
And I think we need
to keep in mind that--

01:37:41.770 --> 01:37:44.680
well, anyway, I don't think
any simple stereotype will

01:37:44.680 --> 01:37:50.160
summarize the ethical thinking
of either the West or the East.

01:37:50.160 --> 01:37:52.130
PINKER: The criticism
is completely valid.

01:37:52.130 --> 01:37:55.380
To put it mildly, talking about
the Judeo-Christian tradition,

01:37:55.380 --> 01:37:59.590
embraces a great many
figures, ideas, and so on,

01:37:59.590 --> 01:38:01.178
so it's bound to be
a simplification.

01:38:01.178 --> 01:38:02.970
I meant it really in
just the minimal sense

01:38:02.970 --> 01:38:06.570
that there's a presumption
that we have an ability

01:38:06.570 --> 01:38:11.520
to measure courses of action
against some ethical standard,

01:38:11.520 --> 01:38:12.790
some coloring.

01:38:12.790 --> 01:38:15.982
And that's probably the most
that you could get away with.

01:38:15.982 --> 01:38:17.940
PUTNAM: Yeah, but the
most common term for that

01:38:17.940 --> 01:38:20.490
was simply reason,
or even common sense.

01:38:20.490 --> 01:38:23.850
[? Savara ?] in the Talmud
is just the general term

01:38:23.850 --> 01:38:24.670
for common sense.

01:38:32.235 --> 01:38:33.700
KEYSER: Yes.

01:38:33.700 --> 01:38:37.460
Andrew, Levinson, and Christy
Keenan have microphones,

01:38:37.460 --> 01:38:39.528
so let's use those.

01:38:39.528 --> 01:38:40.070
AUDIENCE: Hi.

01:38:40.070 --> 01:38:42.800
My question is for
the entire panel,

01:38:42.800 --> 01:38:46.250
but, I guess,
mainly, because I'm

01:38:46.250 --> 01:38:50.400
most familiar with
Professor Chomsky's work.

01:38:50.400 --> 01:38:52.130
In the same vein of
the last question,

01:38:52.130 --> 01:38:54.920
I'm just interested
in each of you,

01:38:54.920 --> 01:38:57.640
what do you use as primary
and secondary sources

01:38:57.640 --> 01:39:00.710
as far as history
sources, current event

01:39:00.710 --> 01:39:05.330
sources, and the organizations
that possibly publish

01:39:05.330 --> 01:39:06.790
these and put these out?

01:39:06.790 --> 01:39:10.160
And the reason is,
is because, I guess,

01:39:10.160 --> 01:39:13.040
with kind of a new
wealth of information,

01:39:13.040 --> 01:39:17.030
it's kind of hard to kind
of mull through this.

01:39:17.030 --> 01:39:20.120
I'm 25, so the
internet's been around

01:39:20.120 --> 01:39:23.690
for a good part of my
lifetime and just inundated

01:39:23.690 --> 01:39:25.410
with a lot of neat information.

01:39:25.410 --> 01:39:29.340
But how much of it is useful
is a different question.

01:39:29.340 --> 01:39:33.110
So I would like to know
what you subscribe to

01:39:33.110 --> 01:39:34.910
and where you get your first--

01:39:34.910 --> 01:39:41.240
the information where--
to analyze what's

01:39:41.240 --> 01:39:42.620
going on in the
world day to day.

01:39:42.620 --> 01:39:44.200
I mean, it's--

01:39:44.200 --> 01:39:45.450
CHOMSKY: Well, there's never--

01:39:45.450 --> 01:39:47.210
I mean, at least
in recent history,

01:39:47.210 --> 01:39:50.000
for those of us who are
reasonably privileged

01:39:50.000 --> 01:39:53.300
and don't have to spend
the entire day trying

01:39:53.300 --> 01:39:55.803
to feed our children
and so on, there's never

01:39:55.803 --> 01:39:57.095
been a shortage of information.

01:39:57.095 --> 01:39:59.670
You're just flooded
with information.

01:39:59.670 --> 01:40:02.760
And the same is true in the
sciences and everywhere else.

01:40:02.760 --> 01:40:07.940
So like a good physicist is not
someone who knows most data.

01:40:07.940 --> 01:40:09.590
In fact, any person--

01:40:09.590 --> 01:40:15.090
probably-- correlation's
probably the opposite.

01:40:15.090 --> 01:40:20.660
I won't mention any names, but
there is one old friend of mine

01:40:20.660 --> 01:40:24.890
who was a Nobel Prize
winning scientist, who

01:40:24.890 --> 01:40:28.520
once said that when he
teaches his courses,

01:40:28.520 --> 01:40:31.860
he makes up the facts
the way they ought to be.

01:40:31.860 --> 01:40:34.700
And then he sort of
challenges the students

01:40:34.700 --> 01:40:37.187
to try to check and
see if he got it right.

01:40:37.187 --> 01:40:38.440
Have them find out.

01:40:38.440 --> 01:40:40.640
And the point that
he was trying to make

01:40:40.640 --> 01:40:46.790
is that the information in
itself is just a nuisance.

01:40:46.790 --> 01:40:50.240
I mean, like the data,
the phenomena of life,

01:40:50.240 --> 01:40:52.550
or a nuisance, you have to
know what questions to ask.

01:40:52.550 --> 01:40:54.133
PUTNAM: This is from
a man who carries

01:40:54.133 --> 01:40:56.146
more information in his head.

01:40:56.146 --> 01:40:58.360
CHOMSKY: No.

01:40:58.360 --> 01:41:00.140
You have to ask the
right questions.

01:41:00.140 --> 01:41:05.030
And there's no, if I may
borrow the word, algorithm,

01:41:05.030 --> 01:41:07.160
for doing that.

01:41:07.160 --> 01:41:08.930
So it really wouldn't
help to list--

01:41:08.930 --> 01:41:11.030
I mean, you could look
at footnotes and things

01:41:11.030 --> 01:41:13.430
we've written, and that'll
tell you what we looked at,

01:41:13.430 --> 01:41:16.400
but you have to find for
yourself what kind of questions

01:41:16.400 --> 01:41:17.810
you want answered.

01:41:17.810 --> 01:41:20.780
And search for answers
to those questions.

01:41:20.780 --> 01:41:25.492
And nobody can tell you
where you'll find them.

01:41:25.492 --> 01:41:26.950
PINKER: I'll put
in a plug for my--

01:41:26.950 --> 01:41:30.800
if I had to name my single
favorite source of information

01:41:30.800 --> 01:41:35.360
for just recreational
information trolling on the web

01:41:35.360 --> 01:41:38.000
would be the pair of sites
called Arts and Letters

01:41:38.000 --> 01:41:43.656
Daily and SciTechDaily,
AlDaily.com, SciTechDaily.com,

01:41:43.656 --> 01:41:47.810
both run by a wonderful
man named Dennis Dutton who

01:41:47.810 --> 01:41:49.800
lives in New Zealand.

01:41:49.800 --> 01:41:53.030
And it's basically
pointers to the three most

01:41:53.030 --> 01:41:55.610
interesting stories
everyday sampled

01:41:55.610 --> 01:42:01.670
from maybe 100 different
journals, magazines, reviews,

01:42:01.670 --> 01:42:03.310
and so on.

01:42:03.310 --> 01:42:07.550
So those would be my two picks.

01:42:07.550 --> 01:42:11.270
PUTNAM: I can improve
on Noam's answer.

01:42:11.270 --> 01:42:15.590
If you bring a good
question to almost any data,

01:42:15.590 --> 01:42:19.350
you'll get a great
deal more out of it.

01:42:19.350 --> 01:42:21.620
KEYSER: If, however, you
go to Google on the net

01:42:21.620 --> 01:42:27.860
and put in Chomsky,
you'll get 113,000 hits.

01:42:27.860 --> 01:42:29.250
Yes.

01:42:29.250 --> 01:42:29.750
Hang on.

01:42:29.750 --> 01:42:31.665
Christie will bring
you a microphone.

01:42:38.100 --> 01:42:40.800
AUDIENCE: I have a
question for Steven Pinker,

01:42:40.800 --> 01:42:43.580
or anybody that
wants to answer that.

01:42:43.580 --> 01:42:47.190
But you touched on in your
talk, and what I want to know

01:42:47.190 --> 01:42:49.680
is how far do you think
the tools of science

01:42:49.680 --> 01:42:52.800
can be used to
explain human nature?

01:42:52.800 --> 01:42:55.350
Are there areas of the arts
and humanities that are just

01:42:55.350 --> 01:42:57.270
out of reach of
scientific tools,

01:42:57.270 --> 01:43:00.630
like cognitive neuroscience,
or behavioral, genetics?

01:43:00.630 --> 01:43:04.450
And I would just
say that I recently

01:43:04.450 --> 01:43:06.450
heard a talk by E. O.
Wilson who says that there

01:43:06.450 --> 01:43:09.780
are biophilic patterns
in art from Picasso

01:43:09.780 --> 01:43:12.450
and other expressionist
paintings that

01:43:12.450 --> 01:43:15.745
are universal in nature.

01:43:15.745 --> 01:43:18.120
PINKER: Well, I think that
the study of cognitive science

01:43:18.120 --> 01:43:22.020
could certainly illuminate
the arts and humanities.

01:43:22.020 --> 01:43:24.780
The plots of the
world's great fiction

01:43:24.780 --> 01:43:28.620
are fairly restricted number
of themes of human conflict

01:43:28.620 --> 01:43:31.770
and human interest.

01:43:31.770 --> 01:43:35.100
Media, like painting and
music, clearly exploit

01:43:35.100 --> 01:43:37.620
properties of the visual
system and the auditory system

01:43:37.620 --> 01:43:40.350
and there's a lot of interesting
work by people like, say,

01:43:40.350 --> 01:43:44.400
Elaine Scarry at Harvard on
how cognitive science can help

01:43:44.400 --> 01:43:50.400
illuminate the fictional
narratives by the use

01:43:50.400 --> 01:43:54.030
of visual imagery, for example.

01:43:54.030 --> 01:43:57.930
Obviously, the study of
literature, arts, humanities

01:43:57.930 --> 01:44:00.690
isn't going to be taken
over by cognitive science

01:44:00.690 --> 01:44:03.240
and evolutionary
psychology, but simply

01:44:03.240 --> 01:44:06.150
because a few principles
can only get you so far,

01:44:06.150 --> 01:44:08.340
but I think there's a
great deal of insight

01:44:08.340 --> 01:44:10.180
to be gained by linking them.

01:44:10.180 --> 01:44:11.680
I'll just mention
one other example,

01:44:11.680 --> 01:44:16.050
which is the work of Jay
Keyser using elements

01:44:16.050 --> 01:44:19.950
of linguistic theory,
the theory of phonology

01:44:19.950 --> 01:44:22.920
to illuminate
contemporary poetry.

01:44:22.920 --> 01:44:25.410
Very interesting work
that shows how fruitful it

01:44:25.410 --> 01:44:28.140
can be to bring
traditional poetics,

01:44:28.140 --> 01:44:32.310
or traditional humanities,
arts, and arts in general

01:44:32.310 --> 01:44:34.920
together with cognitive science.

01:44:34.920 --> 01:44:38.100
PUTNAM: I think, to ask
whether there are problems

01:44:38.100 --> 01:44:41.280
in the humanities and the arts
and so on that are out of reach

01:44:41.280 --> 01:44:43.740
is the wrong way of
thinking about it.

01:44:43.740 --> 01:44:47.125
It's as if all the problems
are scientific problems,

01:44:47.125 --> 01:44:49.500
it assumes that all the problems
are scientific problems,

01:44:49.500 --> 01:44:51.390
and the line we have to--

01:44:51.390 --> 01:44:53.910
the divide we have to make
is in scientific problems

01:44:53.910 --> 01:44:55.800
that are tractable and
scientific problems

01:44:55.800 --> 01:44:57.585
that are untractable.

01:44:57.585 --> 01:45:02.190
Now, obviously, there are within
science both tractable and

01:45:02.190 --> 01:45:03.930
intractable problems,
but there are also

01:45:03.930 --> 01:45:07.130
questions, which are
not scientific at all.

01:45:07.130 --> 01:45:08.810
Their vocabularies, which are--

01:45:08.810 --> 01:45:10.800
their vocabularies,
whose purpose,

01:45:10.800 --> 01:45:19.740
whose reason for existence
isn't to formulate causal laws.

01:45:19.740 --> 01:45:23.490
You mentioned that the
term reductionism is vague,

01:45:23.490 --> 01:45:25.880
as Steve Pinker
pointed out, but there

01:45:25.880 --> 01:45:27.960
is one kind of reductions,
which is certainly

01:45:27.960 --> 01:45:32.100
a fallacy, the
logician Frege, who

01:45:32.100 --> 01:45:36.960
is the founder of modern
symbolic logic, referred

01:45:36.960 --> 01:45:39.210
to psychologism, because
he was thinking of one very

01:45:39.210 --> 01:45:42.050
narrow sort of reductionism.

01:45:42.050 --> 01:45:45.870
In his day, psychology
really meant association

01:45:45.870 --> 01:45:47.763
of ideas, and
especially association

01:45:47.763 --> 01:45:49.680
of mental images, which
is a very limited kind

01:45:49.680 --> 01:45:50.500
of psychology.

01:45:50.500 --> 01:45:51.917
But the point he
was making, which

01:45:51.917 --> 01:45:56.810
is that the question of
whether an inference is valid,

01:45:56.810 --> 01:45:58.540
when something is
really a proof, which

01:45:58.540 --> 01:46:03.040
is why I use this example,
is a wholly different kind

01:46:03.040 --> 01:46:07.210
of question from the empirical
question of what causal

01:46:07.210 --> 01:46:09.730
mechanisms of
association, or whatever,

01:46:09.730 --> 01:46:12.430
went on in the
mind or brain when

01:46:12.430 --> 01:46:14.500
the person gave that proof.

01:46:14.500 --> 01:46:16.390
And to confuse
those two questions

01:46:16.390 --> 01:46:19.850
is to miss, what I call,
the normativity of logic.

01:46:19.850 --> 01:46:21.280
I mean, I am a pluralist.

01:46:21.280 --> 01:46:23.020
I think we have many--

01:46:23.020 --> 01:46:25.420
we need many, many
different language games.

01:46:25.420 --> 01:46:29.185
And no one of our language games
can describe all of reality.

01:46:39.480 --> 01:46:42.398
KEYSER: Am I missing anybody?

01:46:42.398 --> 01:46:45.740
Oh, good.

01:46:45.740 --> 01:46:46.820
AUDIENCE: Yes.

01:46:46.820 --> 01:46:50.420
I'd like to ask somewhat
of an obvious question that

01:46:50.420 --> 01:46:54.940
relates to the subject
of this gathering,

01:46:54.940 --> 01:46:57.710
specifically about
the point of having

01:46:57.710 --> 01:47:02.120
a humanities department in a
technical institute like MIT.

01:47:02.120 --> 01:47:05.450
And I'd wonder if you could
talk a little bit about what

01:47:05.450 --> 01:47:09.530
the role of it is and
whether over the years

01:47:09.530 --> 01:47:14.750
there is a sense of
prejudice against humanists

01:47:14.750 --> 01:47:15.830
versus scientists.

01:47:18.380 --> 01:47:21.400
CHOMSKY: Well, I guess I'm the
one with the longest experience

01:47:21.400 --> 01:47:23.950
here, so let's talk about that.

01:47:23.950 --> 01:47:27.220
When I got here, it
was in the mid 1950s,

01:47:27.220 --> 01:47:31.300
and MIT was basically
an engineering school.

01:47:31.300 --> 01:47:32.980
Even the basic
science departments

01:47:32.980 --> 01:47:35.110
were pretty much
service departments.

01:47:35.110 --> 01:47:38.860
I mean, they had super
scientists in them,

01:47:38.860 --> 01:47:42.100
but their role in the institute
was service departments.

01:47:42.100 --> 01:47:45.310
Actually, I think
I introduced one

01:47:45.310 --> 01:47:50.170
of the first undergraduate
philosophy courses

01:47:50.170 --> 01:47:52.990
in general, philosophy
and analytic philosophy

01:47:52.990 --> 01:47:55.070
back in 1955.

01:47:55.070 --> 01:47:59.560
And I guess it's not
telling a tale out of school

01:47:59.560 --> 01:48:03.280
if I say some of the things
that students who were then

01:48:03.280 --> 01:48:05.200
undergraduates asked me.

01:48:05.200 --> 01:48:08.230
So, for example, I remember
the first philosophy course

01:48:08.230 --> 01:48:11.830
I introduced that students,
undergraduates came

01:48:11.830 --> 01:48:13.810
to ask what the course
was going to be about

01:48:13.810 --> 01:48:15.150
and should they take it.

01:48:15.150 --> 01:48:19.240
And I'll never forget one
student who came and asked me

01:48:19.240 --> 01:48:22.180
whether this would be the
kind of course that would

01:48:22.180 --> 01:48:27.220
help him meet Radcliffe girls.

01:48:27.220 --> 01:48:29.980
Radcliffe was then
separate from Harvard.

01:48:29.980 --> 01:48:32.680
The point was that he had
to go to these parties

01:48:32.680 --> 01:48:36.490
with all these Harvard kids who
were hot shots and new word,

01:48:36.490 --> 01:48:41.030
things like, names, at least,
like Kant and Freud and so on.

01:48:41.030 --> 01:48:43.660
And we just kind of
helped him compete.

01:48:43.660 --> 01:48:46.780
Well, that wasn't so far
from the way things were.

01:48:46.780 --> 01:48:49.250
It changed a lot.

01:48:49.250 --> 01:48:52.970
It changed a lot for
a lot of reasons.

01:48:52.970 --> 01:48:55.030
I mean, one of the reasons
it changed, I think,

01:48:55.030 --> 01:48:58.210
is because of-- a major
reason, was Sputnik.

01:48:58.210 --> 01:49:00.430
I've always had a
sneaking suspicion

01:49:00.430 --> 01:49:04.390
that Sputnik was actually
sent up by US scientists.

01:49:04.390 --> 01:49:06.590
They all knew that it
didn't mean a thing,

01:49:06.590 --> 01:49:09.560
but they exploited
it fantastically

01:49:09.560 --> 01:49:12.940
to get an enormous amount
of money and support

01:49:12.940 --> 01:49:15.130
for science education
and enriching

01:49:15.130 --> 01:49:17.260
the scientific
institutions and so on.

01:49:17.260 --> 01:49:19.300
And one very visible
consequence of this

01:49:19.300 --> 01:49:22.510
was that MIT changed from
an engineering school

01:49:22.510 --> 01:49:25.600
to a science based university.

01:49:25.600 --> 01:49:27.850
And as it became a
science based university,

01:49:27.850 --> 01:49:32.020
there was an increasing
and a quite natural demand

01:49:32.020 --> 01:49:35.500
for enriching the
humanities courses.

01:49:35.500 --> 01:49:39.320
And it sort of grew
naturally out of that.

01:49:39.320 --> 01:49:41.480
So it was certainly since--

01:49:41.480 --> 01:49:43.930
in fact, the whole
service function

01:49:43.930 --> 01:49:45.670
of many of the
departments changed.

01:49:45.670 --> 01:49:49.130
They became core departments,
not service departments,

01:49:49.130 --> 01:49:52.420
including humanities.

01:49:52.420 --> 01:49:53.720
Linguistics was the same.

01:49:53.720 --> 01:49:56.200
I'll just tell you my
own personal story.

01:49:56.200 --> 01:49:59.980
When I got here in
the 1950s, actually,

01:49:59.980 --> 01:50:02.350
I was in a laboratory,
but I had to sort of pay

01:50:02.350 --> 01:50:04.780
my way in the humanities
department, then

01:50:04.780 --> 01:50:06.700
the modern languages department.

01:50:06.700 --> 01:50:12.310
And the way we did
that was by teaching

01:50:12.310 --> 01:50:16.960
cram courses for
graduate students,

01:50:16.960 --> 01:50:19.300
mostly like civil
engineers and others.

01:50:19.300 --> 01:50:22.170
In those days, there was a
kind of crazy requirement

01:50:22.170 --> 01:50:26.740
that to get your PhD,
you had to fake your way

01:50:26.740 --> 01:50:29.230
through language exams.

01:50:29.230 --> 01:50:32.200
And I stress, fake your way,
because it was understood

01:50:32.200 --> 01:50:35.080
by everyone that you
were going to be trained

01:50:35.080 --> 01:50:37.673
to learn how to fake your
way through a particular exam

01:50:37.673 --> 01:50:40.090
and they were never going to
open an article in that field

01:50:40.090 --> 01:50:41.200
again.

01:50:41.200 --> 01:50:43.870
And that was done
for a long time.

01:50:43.870 --> 01:50:46.410
Well, that's what the
language and linguistics

01:50:46.410 --> 01:50:47.680
was at the time.

01:50:47.680 --> 01:50:48.520
And it changed.

01:50:48.520 --> 01:50:50.440
It changed in a
very natural way,

01:50:50.440 --> 01:50:52.510
I think, as the
university shifted

01:50:52.510 --> 01:50:55.870
towards becoming a
science based university

01:50:55.870 --> 01:51:01.780
with a demand and a need for
rich surrounding departments

01:51:01.780 --> 01:51:05.954
that exist on their
own initiative.

01:51:12.730 --> 01:51:14.680
KEYSER: I think
that's a terrific way

01:51:14.680 --> 01:51:15.640
to end the session.

01:51:15.640 --> 01:51:17.818
And I hope you'll all
join me in thanking

01:51:17.818 --> 01:51:20.110
the panel for a terrific
beginning to this celebration.

01:51:20.110 --> 01:51:23.460
[APPLAUSE]

